\subsection{Pre-optimization design}
We hereby show a possible usage of the error bounds determined in Section~\ref{sec:symmetric} to design the architecture skeleton of deep symmetric autoencoders suitably initialized with the EYS procedure, before executing any kind of architecture optimization. This is a critical advantage from a computational viewpoint because in principle it avoids repeating possibly expensive training routines to tune the hyperparameters. Specifically, in the following, we aim to characterize the dimension of the hidden levels, having fixed the latent dimension according to the criterion proposed by Franco et al. \cite{franco2023deep}. To this end, we initialize a batch of two-level \textsf{SAE}s, varying both their sharpness and the first level dimension $n_1$, and evaluate the MSE and the error bounds accordingly. We report the results in Fig.  , through which we empirically validate that 
\begin{itemize}
    \item[-] both the upper bound and the MSE are increasing as function of $n_1$, and therefore cannot be used for designing the hidden levels dimension. Note that, on the other hand, the MSE is effective for characterizing the latent dimension $n_2$ (viz. Fig.~\ref{fig:init_study}). 
    \item[-] the lower bound is monotone decreasing and reaches a plateau at $n_1 = 11$. Thus, in the wake of SVD-based methods, heuristically, this leads us to select $n_1 > 11$ to suitably capture the manifold variance. 
    \item[-] sharper activation functions promote faster decay of the lower bound and smaller plateau values. Still, if the sharpness is excessively large, the starting MSE value may explode (see Figs. ), thus often leading to training convergence issues. For this reason, domain practitioners should find a suitable balance between these two factors.
\end{itemize} 
