{\bf Sensitivity Analyses Under Various EE Strategies:}  
Fig.~\ref{fig:drl-sens-ee} examines the effects of true information propagation, network observability, and users' prior beliefs on EE strategies. Both TP and FP employ DRL-based agents for seed selection, allowing evaluation against ``smart" opponents. Due to space constraints, results for the URV Email dataset are shown, while analyses for FB networks appear in Appendix B.3 of the supplement.


Fig.~\ref{fig:drl-drl-ee-IP-sens-ds1} shows that all EE strategies gain influence as information propagations (IP) increase from 1 to 3, confirming that additional propagation enhances effectiveness. Fig.~\ref{fig:drl-drl-PON-sens-ds1} reveals a significant boost for all strategies as network observability rises from 0.7 to 1, underscoring their reliance on visibility. VAC-EE and VD-EE improve steeply, excelling in well-observed networks. Fig.~\ref{fig:drl-drl-base-rate-sens-ds1} explores users' prior beliefs. As noted in Section~\ref{subsec:cim-PA}, high user activity mitigates belief influence. Still, VAC-EE and VD-EE outperform others, benefiting from true information acceptance and low uncertainty. DIS-EE and ENT-EE remain stable, suggesting greater dependence on network structure than user biases.

