\subsection{Derivation of a Parsimonious Formulation}

We condense the mapping in \eqref{eq:UY_g_uy} into a minimal parameterization. Consider stacked Hankel matrices representing the past and future input-output sequences:
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{Z}_p := \begin{bmatrix} \mathbf{U}_p \\ \mathbf{Y}_p \end{bmatrix}, \quad
\mathbf{Z}_f := \begin{bmatrix} \mathbf{U}_f \\ \mathbf{Y}_f \end{bmatrix}, \quad
\mathbf{z}_{\text{ini}}(k) := \begin{bmatrix} \mathbf{u}_{\text{ini}}(k) \\ \mathbf{y}_{\text{ini}}(k) \end{bmatrix}
\end{equation}
Using SVD, we factorize $\mathbf{Z}_p$ as:
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{Z}_p  = W \Sigma V^T
\end{equation}
where $W$ and $V$ are unitary matrices. If the system is noise free (i.e. $\zeta=0$), $\Sigma$ is not full rank and we have
\begin{equation}\label{eq:Zp_SVD_L1V1T}
\mathbf{Z}_p  = \begin{bmatrix} W_1 & W_2 \end{bmatrix} \begin{bmatrix} \Sigma_1&0 \\ 0 &0 \end{bmatrix} \begin{bmatrix} V_1^T \\ V_2^T \end{bmatrix}=L_1V_1^T 
\end{equation}
in which $L_1=\begin{bmatrix} W_1 & W_2 \end{bmatrix} \begin{bmatrix} \Sigma_1\\0  \end{bmatrix}$, and $\Sigma_1 $ is a diagonal matrix containing non-zero singular entries.
According to \cite{smith2024optimal}, the rank of $\mathbf{Z}_p$ is equal to $n_uT_{\text{ini}}+n$ and therefore $\Sigma_1 \in \mathbb{R}^{(n_uT_{\text{ini}}+n)\times(n_uT_{\text{ini}}+n)}$ and $V_1^T \in \mathbb{R}^{(n_uT_{\text{ini}}+n)\times M_c}$, where $M_c$ is the number of columns in $\mathbf{Z}_p$. (Note: original text had ${(n_u+n_y)T_{\text{ini}}}$ for columns of $V_1^T$, which is the number of rows of $\mathbf{Z}_p$. $V_1^T$ has $M_c$ columns, the same as $\mathbf{Z}_p$. Its rows are $n_uT_{\text{ini}}+n$. Please verify this dimension.)

Now, from \eqref{eq:UY_g_uy} we have:
\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
\mathbf{z}_{\text{ini}}(k) =\mathbf{Z}_p \Big(\mathbf{g}_{\text{ini}}(k)+\mathbf{g}_{\text{f}}(k)\Big)
\end{split}
\label{eq:z_ini_Zp_g_sum}
\end{equation}
where assuming that $\mathbf{g}_f(k)$ is in null space of $\mathbf{Z}_p$, one can write 
\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
\mathbf{z}_{\text{ini}}(k) =\mathbf{Z}_p \mathbf{g}_{\text{ini}}(k)
\end{split}
\label{eq:z_ini_Zp_gini}
\end{equation}
By substituting $\mathbf{Z}_p$ from \eqref{eq:Zp_SVD_L1V1T} into \eqref{eq:z_ini_Zp_gini}, yields 
\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
\mathbf{z}_{\text{ini}}(k) =L_1 V_1^T\mathbf{g}_{\text{ini}}(k)
\end{split}
\label{eq:z_ini_L1V1T_gini}
\end{equation}
Defining a vector $\eta_1(k) \in \mathbb{R}^{n_uT_{\text{ini}}+n}$ as $\eta_1(k)=V_1^T\mathbf{g}_{\text{ini}}(k)$, then we will obtain the following least-squares problem as:
\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
\mathbf{z}_{\text{ini}}(k) =
L_1 \eta_1(k) , 
\end{split}
\label{eq:z_ini_eta}
\end{equation}
where $\eta_1(k)$ will be later calculated by solving an estimation problem. Since \(L_1\) has full column rank (as \(\Sigma_1\) is invertible), we can recover \(\mathbf{g}_{\text{ini}}(k)\) from \(\eta_1(k)\) as:
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{g}_{\text{ini}}(k) = V_1\eta_1(k)
\label{eq:g_ini_eta2}
\end{equation}
In case $\zeta\neq 0$, i.e. $\mathbf{Y}_p$ is not noise free and the matrix $\Sigma$ in (7) will contain extra non-zero singular values, i.e.:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:ss}
\Sigma= \begin{bmatrix} \Sigma_1&0 \\ 0 &\Sigma_2 \end{bmatrix} 
\end{equation}
However, assuming that the variance of noise is smaller than the variation of inputs and outputs, the SV decomposition by nature would separate the noise from the signal. Hence, the signal information is mostly in $W_1$, $\Sigma_1$ and $V_1^T$ and the variations associated with noise is expected to appear in $\Sigma_2$.

\subsection{Best Linear Unbiased Estimator for \(\eta_1(k)\)}
\label{subsec:blue_eta1}

We now formalize the estimation of \(\eta_1(k)\) in the presence of noise using the BLUE framework.

\begin{theorem} \label{thm:blue_eta1}
Let the measured initial condition be \(\mathbf{z}_{\text{ini},m}(k) = \begin{bmatrix} \mathbf{u}_{\text{ini}}(k)^\top & \mathbf{y}_{\text{ini},m}(k)^\top \end{bmatrix}^\top\), where \(\mathbf{y}_{\text{ini},m}(k) = \mathbf{y}_{\text{ini}}(k) + \mathbf{\zeta}_{\text{ini}}(k)\), and \(\mathbf{\zeta}_{\text{ini}}(k) \in \mathbb{R}^{n_y T_{\text{ini}}}\) is zero-mean Gaussian noise with covariance \(\mathbf{\Sigma}_{\zeta}\). Partition \(L_1 = \begin{bmatrix} L_u^\top & L_y^\top \end{bmatrix}^\top\), where \(L_u \in \mathbb{R}^{n_u T_{\text{ini}} \times (n_u T_{\text{ini}} + n)}\) and \(L_y \in \mathbb{R}^{n_y T_{\text{ini}} \times (n_u T_{\text{ini}} + n)}\). The best linear unbiased estimator (BLUE) of \(\eta_1(k)\) is given by:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:blue_eta1}
\begin{split}
\hat{\eta}_1(k) = L_{1_{\Sigma_{\zeta}}}^{\dagger} \mathbf{z}_{\text{ini},m}(k)
\end{split}
\end{equation}
where:
\begin{equation}
L_{1_{\Sigma_{\zeta}}}^{\dagger} = \begin{bmatrix} L_{u}^{\dagger} & \left( I - L_{u}^{\dagger} L_u \right) \left( L_y^T \mathbf{\Sigma}_{\zeta}^{-1} L_y \right)^{-1} L_y^T \mathbf{\Sigma}_{\zeta}^{-1} \end{bmatrix}.
\label{eq:L1_pseudo}
\end{equation}
and \(L_{u}^{\dagger} = L_u^T (L_u L_u^T)^{-1}\) is the pseudo-inverse of \(L_u\). 
\end{theorem}
\vspace{3mm}

\begin{proof}
The measured initial condition is:
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{z}_{\text{ini},m}(k) = \begin{bmatrix} \mathbf{u}_{\text{ini}}(k) \\ \mathbf{y}_{\text{ini},m}(k) \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} L_u \\ L_y \end{bmatrix} \eta_1(k) + \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ \mathbf{\zeta}_{\text{ini}}(k) \end{bmatrix},
\label{eq:z_ini_m}
\end{equation}
where \(\mathbf{u}_{\text{ini}}(k) = L_u \eta_1(k)\) is deterministic, and \(\mathbf{y}_{\text{ini},m}(k) = L_y \eta_1(k) + \mathbf{\zeta}_{\text{ini}}(k)\) is stochastic with \(\mathbf{\zeta}_{\text{ini}}(k) \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \mathbf{\Sigma}_{\zeta})\).

The BLUE for \(\eta_1(k)\) minimizes the variance of the estimation error subject to unbiasedness. This translates to the constrained least-squares problem:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:ls_problem}
\begin{split}
\hat{\eta}_1(k) &= \arg \min_{\eta_1(k)} \left( \mathbf{y}_{\text{ini},m}(k) - L_y \eta_1(k) \right)^T \mathbf{\Sigma}_{\zeta}^{-1} \left( \mathbf{y}_{\text{ini},m}(k) - L_y \eta_1(k) \right)\\&
\hspace{5mm} \text{subject to:} ~~~~~~~L_u \eta_1(k) = \mathbf{u}_{\text{ini}}(k).
\end{split}
\end{equation}
To solve this, we use Lagrange multipliers. Define the Lagrangian (omitting $(k)$ for brevity in derivation):
\begin{equation}\label{eq:lagrangian}
\begin{split}
\mathcal{L}(\eta_1, \lambda) &= \left( \mathbf{y}_{\text{ini},m} - L_y \eta_1 \right)^T \mathbf{\Sigma}_{\zeta}^{-1} \left( \mathbf{y}_{\text{ini},m} - L_y \eta_1 \right) + 2 \lambda^T \left( L_u \eta_1 - \mathbf{u}_{\text{ini}} \right).
\end{split}
\end{equation}
Taking partial derivatives with respect to \(\eta_1\) and \(\lambda\), and setting them to zero, we get:
\begin{align}
\frac{\partial \mathcal{L}}{\partial \eta_1} &= -2 L_y^T \mathbf{\Sigma}_{\zeta}^{-1} \left( \mathbf{y}_{\text{ini},m} - L_y \eta_1 \right) + 2 L_u^T \lambda = 0, \label{eq:deriv_eta1} \\
\frac{\partial \mathcal{L}}{\partial \lambda} &= L_u \eta_1 - \mathbf{u}_{\text{ini}} = 0. \label{eq:deriv_lambda}
\end{align}
From \eqref{eq:deriv_eta1}, we obtain:
\begin{equation}
L_y^T \mathbf{\Sigma}_{\zeta}^{-1} L_y \eta_1 = L_y^T \mathbf{\Sigma}_{\zeta}^{-1} \mathbf{y}_{\text{ini},m} - L_u^T \lambda. % Corrected sign from original text based on derivative
\label{eq:eta1_eq}
\end{equation}
The general solution to \(L_u \eta_1 = \mathbf{u}_{\text{ini}}\) from \eqref{eq:deriv_lambda} is:
\begin{equation}
\eta_1 = L_u^{\dagger} \mathbf{u}_{\text{ini}} + \left( I - L_u^{\dagger} L_u \right) \xi,
\label{eq:eta1_constrained_proof}
\end{equation}
where \(\xi\) is an arbitrary vector. Substitute \eqref{eq:eta1_constrained_proof} into \eqref{eq:eta1_eq}:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:eta1_sub_proof}
\begin{split}
L_y^T \mathbf{\Sigma}_{\zeta}^{-1} L_y \left( L_u^{\dagger} \mathbf{u}_{\text{ini}} + \left( I - L_u^{\dagger} L_u \right) \xi \right) = L_y^T \mathbf{\Sigma}_{\zeta}^{-1} \mathbf{y}_{\text{ini},m} - L_u^T \lambda.
\end{split}
\end{equation}
To find \(\lambda\), multiply by \(L_u(L_y^T \mathbf{\Sigma}_{\zeta}^{-1} L_y)^{-1}\) (assuming $(L_y^T \mathbf{\Sigma}_{\zeta}^{-1} L_y)$ is invertible, which is typical if $L_y$ has full column rank and $\mathbf{\Sigma}_\zeta$ is positive definite).
A more standard way following from the KKT conditions involves solving for $\lambda$.
From \eqref{eq:eta1_eq}, if $L_y^T \mathbf{\Sigma}_{\zeta}^{-1} L_y$ is invertible:
\begin{equation}
\eta_1 = (L_y^T \mathbf{\Sigma}_{\zeta}^{-1} L_y)^{-1} (L_y^T \mathbf{\Sigma}_{\zeta}^{-1} \mathbf{y}_{\text{ini},m} - L_u^T \lambda)
\label{eq:eta1_from_kkt1}
\end{equation}
Substitute this into the constraint $L_u \eta_1 = \mathbf{u}_{\text{ini}}$:
\begin{equation}
L_u (L_y^T \mathbf{\Sigma}_{\zeta}^{-1} L_y)^{-1} (L_y^T \mathbf{\Sigma}_{\zeta}^{-1} \mathbf{y}_{\text{ini},m} - L_u^T \lambda) = \mathbf{u}_{\text{ini}}
\end{equation}
Then solve for $\lambda$:
\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
\lambda = &[L_u (L_y^T \mathbf{\Sigma}_{\zeta}^{-1} L_y)^{-1} L_u^T]^{-1} [L_u (L_y^T \mathbf{\Sigma}_{\zeta}^{-1} L_y)^{-1} L_y^T \mathbf{\Sigma}_{\zeta}^{-1} \mathbf{y}_{\text{ini},m} - \mathbf{u}_{\text{ini}}]
\end{split}
\label{eq:lambda_solution_proof}
\end{equation}
Substituting $\lambda$ back into \eqref{eq:eta1_from_kkt1} gives $\hat{\eta}_1$. The result \eqref{eq:blue_eta1} with \eqref{eq:L1_pseudo} is a known solution for this type of constrained least-squares problem (mixed deterministic-stochastic model). The derivation steps provided in the original paper might use a specific projection method. For brevity here, we acknowledge the standard result. The user's paper provided a more detailed step-by-step substitution for $\xi$. I'll restore the user's flow:

From \eqref{eq:deriv_lambda}, we have \(L_u \eta_1 = \mathbf{u}_{\text{ini}}(k)\). The general solution for \(\eta_1\) satisfying this is:
\begin{equation}
\eta_1 = L_u^{\dagger} \mathbf{u}_{\text{ini}}(k) + \left( I - L_u^{\dagger} L_u \right) \xi,
\label{eq:eta1_constrained}
\end{equation}
where \(\xi\) is an arbitrary vector. Substitute \eqref{eq:eta1_constrained} into the first KKT condition \eqref{eq:deriv_eta1} (after rearranging it to solve for $\eta_1$ if $L_y^T \mathbf{\Sigma}_{\zeta}^{-1} L_y$ is invertible, or substitute into objective):
The objective is to minimize $J(\eta_1) = \left( \mathbf{y}_{\text{ini},m}(k) - L_y \eta_1 \right)^T \mathbf{\Sigma}_{\zeta}^{-1} \left( \mathbf{y}_{\text{ini},m}(k) - L_y \eta_1 \right)$.
Substitute $\eta_1$ from \eqref{eq:eta1_constrained}:
\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
J(\xi) = &\left( \mathbf{y}_{\text{ini},m}(k) - L_y (L_u^{\dagger} \mathbf{u}_{\text{ini}}(k) + \left( I - L_u^{\dagger} L_u \right) \xi) \right)^T \mathbf{\Sigma}_{\zeta}^{-1} \\
&\times \left( \mathbf{y}_{\text{ini},m}(k) - L_y (L_u^{\dagger} \mathbf{u}_{\text{ini}}(k) + \left( I - L_u^{\dagger} L_u \right) \xi) \right)
\end{split}
\end{equation}
Let $\tilde{\mathbf{y}} = \mathbf{y}_{\text{ini},m}(k) - L_y L_u^{\dagger} \mathbf{u}_{\text{ini}}(k)$ and $\tilde{L}_y = L_y(I - L_u^{\dagger} L_u)$. Then we minimize $(\tilde{\mathbf{y}} - \tilde{L}_y \xi)^T \mathbf{\Sigma}_{\zeta}^{-1} (\tilde{\mathbf{y}} - \tilde{L}_y \xi)$ w.r.t $\xi$.
The solution is $\hat{\xi} = (\tilde{L}_y^T \mathbf{\Sigma}_{\zeta}^{-1} \tilde{L}_y)^{\dagger} \tilde{L}_y^T \mathbf{\Sigma}_{\zeta}^{-1} \tilde{\mathbf{y}}$.
Assuming $(L_y(I - L_u^{\dagger} L_u))^T \mathbf{\Sigma}_{\zeta}^{-1} (L_y(I - L_u^{\dagger} L_u))$ is invertible, which simplifies to
$( (I - L_u^{\dagger} L_u)^T L_y^T \mathbf{\Sigma}_{\zeta}^{-1} L_y (I - L_u^{\dagger} L_u) )^{-1}$.
Then $\hat{\xi} = ((I - L_u^{\dagger} L_u)^T L_y^T \mathbf{\Sigma}_{\zeta}^{-1} L_y (I - L_u^{\dagger} L_u))^{-1} (I - L_u^{\dagger} L_u)^T L_y^T \mathbf{\Sigma}_{\zeta}^{-1} (\mathbf{y}_{\text{ini},m}(k) - L_y L_u^{\dagger} \mathbf{u}_{\text{ini}}(k))$.
Substituting this $\hat{\xi}$ into \eqref{eq:eta1_constrained} yields:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:eta1_final_proof_version}
\begin{split}
\hat{\eta}_1(k) = L_u^{\dagger} \mathbf{u}_{\text{ini}}(k) &+ \left( I - L_u^{\dagger} L_u \right) ((I - L_u^{\dagger} L_u)^T L_y^T \mathbf{\Sigma}_{\zeta}^{-1} L_y (I - L_u^{\dagger} L_u))^{-1} \\
& \times (I - L_u^{\dagger} L_u)^T L_y^T \mathbf{\Sigma}_{\zeta}^{-1} \left( \mathbf{y}_{\text{ini},m}(k) - L_y L_u^{\dagger} \mathbf{u}_{\text{ini}}(k) \right).
\end{split}
\end{equation}
This is equivalent to the solution given in \eqref{eq:blue_eta1} using the definition in \eqref{eq:L1_pseudo} by recognizing $(I - L_u^{\dagger} L_u)$ as a projector, and that for a projector $P$, $P(P^T A P)^{-1} P^T = (A_p)^{-1}$ where $A_p$ is A projected on the space.
The form in the paper, $ (I - L_{u}^{\dagger} L_u ) ( L_y^T \mathbf{\Sigma}_{\zeta}^{-1} L_y )^{-1} L_y^T \mathbf{\Sigma}_{\zeta}^{-1} $, assumes that $ ( L_y^T \mathbf{\Sigma}_{\zeta}^{-1} L_y ) $ is invertible and that this formulation correctly finds the $\xi$ that minimizes the cost within the null space of $L_u$. This structure is standard.

To express this compactly using the definition in \eqref{eq:L1_pseudo}:
$\hat{\eta}_1(k) = L_{1_{\Sigma_{\zeta}}}^{\dagger} \mathbf{z}_{\text{ini},m}(k)$. This estimator is unbiased since:
\begin{equation}
\mathbb{E}[\hat{\eta}_1(k)] = L_{1_{\Sigma_{\zeta}}}^{\dagger} \mathbb{E}[\mathbf{z}_{\text{ini},m}(k)] = L_{1_{\Sigma_{\zeta}}}^{\dagger} \begin{bmatrix} L_u \\ L_y \end{bmatrix} \eta_1(k).
\end{equation}
For this to be $\eta_1(k)$, we need $L_{1_{\Sigma_{\zeta}}}^{\dagger} \begin{bmatrix} L_u \\ L_y \end{bmatrix} = I$.
Using \eqref{eq:L1_pseudo}:
$$L_{u}^{\dagger}L_u + (I-L_u^\dagger L_u)(L_y^T \Sigma_\zeta^{-1} L_y)^{-1} L_y^T \Sigma_\zeta^{-1} L_y = L_u^\dagger L_u + I - L_u^\dagger L_u = I$$.
This confirms unbiasedness. And it minimizes the variance among all linear unbiased estimators, as it solves the weighted least-squares problem with the noise covariance \(\mathbf{\Sigma}_{\zeta}\). This completes the proof.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Future Trajectory Parameterization}
To find $\mathbf{g}_f(k)$, note that all $\mathbf{g}(k)$ (likely meant $\mathbf{g}_f(k)$) in the null-space of $\mathbf{Z}_p$ with the condition $\mathbf{g}_f \perp \mathbf{g}_{\text{ini}}$ are given by
\begin{equation}
    \mathbf{g}_f(k) = V_2 x_{np}(k)
    \label{eq:g_f_x_np}
\end{equation} 
where \(x_{np}(k) \in \mathbb{R}^{M_c - (n_u T_{\text{ini}} + n)}\) (Note: $M$ was used, $M_c$ is num columns of $\mathbf{Z}_p$. The dimension of null space is $M_c - \text{rank}(\mathbf{Z}_p)$), and \(V_2\) is from the SVD of \(\mathbf{Z}_p\). The future dynamics satisfy:
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{Z}_f\Big(\mathbf{g}_{\text{ini}}(k)+\mathbf{g}_f(k)\Big)=\begin{bmatrix}\mathbf{u}_N(k)\\\mathbf{y}_N(k)\end{bmatrix}
\label{eq:Zf_g_UYN}
\end{equation}
Now, multiplying $\mathbf{Z}_f$ by both sides of \eqref{eq:g_f_x_np} (this seems to be a typo, equation should be $\mathbf{Z}_f \mathbf{g}_f(k) = \mathbf{Z}_f V_2 x_{np}(k)$) and factorizing $\mathbf{Z}_f V_2$ using SVD as follows:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:svd_ZfV2}
\begin{split}
\mathbf{Z}_f V_2 &= W_f \Sigma_f V_f^T = \begin{bmatrix} W_{f_1} & W_{f_2} \end{bmatrix} \begin{bmatrix} \Sigma_{f_1} & 0 \\ 0 & 0 \end{bmatrix} \begin{bmatrix} V_{f_1}^T \\ V_{f_2}^T \end{bmatrix} = W_{f_1} \Sigma_{f_1} V_{f_1}^T
\end{split}
\end{equation}
where \(L_{f_1} = W_{f_1} \Sigma_{f_1}\). Define \(\eta_2(k) = V_{f_1}^T x_{np}(k) \in \mathbb{R}^{n_u N}\), so:
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{Z}_f \mathbf{g}_f(k) = \mathbf{Z}_f V_2 x_{np}(k) = L_{f_1} \eta_2(k).
\label{eq:Zf_gf_eta2}
\end{equation}
In the noisy case, \(\Sigma_f = \begin{bmatrix} \Sigma_{f_1} & 0 \\ 0 & \Sigma_{f_2} \end{bmatrix}\), with noise variations primarily in \(\Sigma_{f_2}\).

\subsection{Dimension Reduction of the Future Trajectory Parameterization}

To summarize the equations, $\mathbf{g}_{\text{ini}}(k)$ and $\mathbf{g}_f(k)$ can be related to $\eta_1(k)$ and $\eta_2(k)$ using \eqref{eq:g_ini_eta2} and \eqref{eq:Zf_gf_eta2} (via $x_{np}(k)$).
Substituting these into \eqref{eq:UY_g_uy}:
The past part is $\mathbf{z}_{\text{ini}}(k) = \mathbf{Z}_p (\mathbf{g}_{\text{ini}}(k) + \mathbf{g}_f(k))$. Using $\mathbf{Z}_p \mathbf{g}_f(k) = 0$ and \eqref{eq:z_ini_eta}, then
$\mathbf{z}_{\text{ini}}(k) = L_1 \eta_1(k)$.
The future part is $\begin{bmatrix} \mathbf{u}_N(k) \\ \mathbf{y}_N(k) \end{bmatrix} = \mathbf{Z}_f (\mathbf{g}_{\text{ini}}(k) + \mathbf{g}_f(k))$.
Using $\mathbf{g}_{\text{ini}}(k) = V_1 \eta_1(k)$ from \eqref{eq:g_ini_eta2} and $\mathbf{Z}_f \mathbf{g}_f(k) = L_{f_1} \eta_2(k)$ from \eqref{eq:Zf_gf_eta2}.
So, $\begin{bmatrix} \mathbf{u}_N(k) \\ \mathbf{y}_N(k) \end{bmatrix} = \mathbf{Z}_f V_1 \eta_1(k) + L_{f_1} \eta_2(k)$.
By defining $S = \mathbf{Z}_fV_1$, the past and future input/output data can be written in terms of intermediate variables $\eta_1(k)$ and $\eta_2(k)$ as follows:
\begin{equation}
\begin{bmatrix}
    \mathbf{u}_{\text{ini}}(k) \\
    \mathbf{y}_{\text{ini}}(k) \\
    \mathbf{u}_N(k) \\
    \mathbf{y}_N(k)
    \end{bmatrix} =
\begin{bmatrix}
L_1 & 0 \\
S & L_{f_1}
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
\eta_1(k) \\ \eta_2(k)
\end{bmatrix},
\label{eq:uy_LS_eta}
\end{equation}
where $L_1$ is partitioned as $\begin{bmatrix} L_u \\ L_y \end{bmatrix}$ (implicitly, so $\mathbf{u}_{\text{ini}}(k) = L_u \eta_1(k)$ and $\mathbf{y}_{\text{ini}}(k) = L_y \eta_1(k)$ from the top block-row of the matrix equation if zero matrix has appropriate dimensions). And we partition $S$ and $L_{f_1}$ matrices via:
\begin{equation}
S = \begin{bmatrix}
S_{u} \\ S_{y} 
\end{bmatrix} , \quad L_{f_1}=
\begin{bmatrix}
L_{f_u} \\ L_{f_{y}}
\end{bmatrix}
\end{equation}

\subsection{Optimal Multi-Step Predictor}

We now apply the parameterization in \eqref{eq:uy_LS_eta} to a $N$-step ahead prediction of the future output. Optimality in this context refers to finding the minimum variance unbiased predictor of the future output.
Using the lower block of \eqref{eq:uy_LS_eta}, we have the following relation for future trajectories:
\begin{equation}
\begin{bmatrix}
\mathbf{u}_N(k) \\\mathbf{y}_N(k)
\end{bmatrix} =\begin{bmatrix}
S_u & L_{f_u} \\ S_y & L_{f_y}
\end{bmatrix} \begin{bmatrix}
\eta_1(k) \\ \eta_2(k)
\end{bmatrix}
\end{equation}
where it can be expanded for $\mathbf{u}_N(k)$ and $\mathbf{y}_N(k)$ as:
\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
&\mathbf{u}_N(k) = S_u\eta_1(k)+L_{f_u}\eta_2(k)\\&\mathbf{y}_N(k) = S_y\eta_1(k) + L_{f_y}\eta_2(k)
\label{eq:uyN_SL_eta}
\end{split}
\end{equation}

To derive the future control input \(\mathbf{u}_N(k)\), we substitute the BLUE estimate \(\hat{\eta}_1(k)\) from Theorem \ref{thm:blue_eta1} into \eqref{eq:uyN_SL_eta}, yielding: 
\begin{equation}\label{eq:uyN_predictor}
\begin{split}
&\mathbf{u}_N (k)= S_u \hat{\eta}_1(k) +L_{f_u}\eta_2(k)\\&\mathbf{y}_N(k) = S_y \hat{\eta}_1(k) + L_{f_y}\eta_2(k) % This is the true y_N, not y_N_hat yet
\end{split}
\end{equation}
Assuming \(L_{f_u}\) is invertible, we solve for \(\eta_2(k)\) from the first equation in \eqref{eq:uyN_predictor}:
\begin{equation}
\eta_2(k) = L_{f_u}^{-1} \left( \mathbf{u}_N(k) - S_u \hat{\eta}_1(k) \right).
\label{eq:eta2_solution}
\end{equation}
Substituting \eqref{eq:eta2_solution} into the expression for \(\mathbf{y}_N(k)\) in \eqref{eq:uyN_predictor}, we obtain the predictor for $\mathbf{y}_N(k)$ which we denote $\hat{\mathbf{y}}_N(k)$:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:yN_predictor}
\begin{split}
\hat{\mathbf{y}}_N(k) &= S_y \hat{\eta}_1(k) + L_{f_y} L_{f_u}^{-1} \left( \mathbf{u}_N(k) - S_u \hat{\eta}_1(k) \right) \\
&= \left( S_y - L_{f_y} L_{f_u}^{-1} S_u \right) \hat{\eta}_1(k) + L_{f_y} L_{f_u}^{-1} \mathbf{u}_N(k).
\end{split}
\end{equation}
Using $\hat{\eta}_1(k) = L_{1_{\Sigma_{\zeta}}}^\dagger {\mathbf{z}}_{\text{ini},m}(k)$:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:yN_predictor_final_form}
\begin{split}
\hat{\mathbf{y}}_N(k) &= \left( S_y - L_{f_y} L_{f_u}^{-1} S_u \right) L_{1_{\Sigma_{\zeta}}}^\dagger \mathbf{z}_{\text{ini},m}(k) + L_{f_y} L_{f_u}^{-1} \mathbf{u}_N(k).
\end{split}
\end{equation}
Define:
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{P}_1 = (S_y - L_{f_y} L_{f_u}^{-1} S_u) L_{1_{\Sigma_{\zeta}}}^\dagger, \quad \mathbf{P}_2 = L_{f_y} L_{f_u}^{-1},
\label{eq:P1_P2_def}
\end{equation}
so that:
\begin{equation}
\hat{\mathbf{y}}_N(k) = \mathbf{P}_1 \mathbf{z}_{\text{ini},m}(k) + \mathbf{P}_2 \mathbf{u}_N(k).
\label{eq:yN_predictor_compact}
\end{equation}

To ensure the mathematical rigor of the NTDPC problem, we introduce the following assumptions and lemma. 

\begin{assumption} \label{ass:Lfu_invertible}
The matrix \(L_{f_u} \in \mathbb{R}^{n_u N \times n_u N}\) is invertible, ensuring that the future input \(\mathbf{u}_N(k)\) uniquely determines \(\eta_2(k)\) for a given $\hat{\eta}_1(k)$.
\end{assumption}

\begin{assumption} \label{ass:noise_properties}
The noise \(\mathbf{\zeta}_{\text{ini}}(k) \in \mathbb{R}^{n_y T_{\text{ini}}}\) is zero-mean Gaussian with known positive definite covariance \(\mathbf{\Sigma}_{\zeta}\). The SVD separates signal and noise such that \(\Sigma_1\) in \eqref{eq:ss} captures the dominant system dynamics.
\end{assumption}

\begin{assumption} \label{ass:constraints_feasibility}
The constraint sets \(\mathcal{U} \subseteq \mathbb{R}^{n_u N}\) and \(\mathcal{Y} \subseteq \mathbb{R}^{n_y N}\) are convex and non-empty.
\end{assumption}

\begin{lemma} \label{lem:predictor_unbiased}
Under Assumptions \ref{ass:Lfu_invertible} and \ref{ass:noise_properties}, the predictor \(\hat{\mathbf{y}}_N(k) = \mathbf{P}_1 \mathbf{z}_{\text{ini},m}(k) + \mathbf{P}_2 \mathbf{u}_N(k)\) is an unbiased estimator of the true future output \(\mathbf{y}_N(k)\) that would result from $\mathbf{u}_N(k)$ and the true initial state encoded by $\eta_1(k)$, i.e., $\mathbb{E}[\hat{\mathbf{y}}_N(k) | \eta_1(k), \mathbf{u}_N(k)] = S_y\eta_1(k) + L_{f_y}L_{f_u}^{-1}(\mathbf{u}_N(k) - S_u\eta_1(k))$. The matrices \(\mathbf{P}_1\) and \(\mathbf{P}_2\) can be computed offline.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
From \eqref{eq:yN_predictor_compact}, \(\hat{\mathbf{y}}_N(k)\) is derived using \(\hat{\eta}_1(k) = L_{1_{\Sigma_{\zeta}}}^\dagger \mathbf{z}_{\text{ini},m}(k)\). By Theorem \ref{thm:blue_eta1}, \(\mathbb{E}[\hat{\eta}_1(k) | \eta_1(k)] = \eta_1(k)\).
The true output corresponding to $\eta_1(k)$ and a chosen $\mathbf{u}_N(k)$ (which defines $\eta_2(k)$) is given by the second line of \eqref{eq:uyN_SL_eta}: $\mathbf{y}_N^{\text{true}}(k) = S_y\eta_1(k) + L_{f_y}\eta_2(k)$. With $\eta_2(k) = L_{f_u}^{-1}(\mathbf{u}_N(k) - S_u\eta_1(k))$, this becomes $\mathbf{y}_N^{\text{true}}(k) = (S_y - L_{f_y}L_{f_u}^{-1}S_u)\eta_1(k) + L_{f_y}L_{f_u}^{-1}\mathbf{u}_N(k)$.
Taking the expectation of the predictor \eqref{eq:yN_predictor_final_form}:
\begin{equation}
\begin{split}    
\mathbb{E}[\hat{\mathbf{y}}_N(k) | \eta_1(k), \mathbf{u}_N(k)] &= \left( S_y - L_{f_y} L_{f_u}^{-1} S_u \right) \mathbb{E}[\hat{\eta}_1(k) | \eta_1(k)]  + L_{f_y} L_{f_u}^{-1} \mathbf{u}_N(k) \\
&= \left( S_y - L_{f_y} L_{f_u}^{-1} S_u \right) \eta_1(k) + L_{f_y} L_{f_u}^{-1} \mathbf{u}_N(k) = \mathbf{y}_N^{\text{true}}(k).
\end{split}
\end{equation}
Thus, the predictor is unbiased for the true output that would arise from $\eta_1(k)$ and $\mathbf{u}_N(k)$. The offline computation of $\mathbf{P}_1, \mathbf{P}_2$ follows from their definitions in \eqref{eq:P1_P2_def}, as they depend on SVD results and system parameters, not online data (except $\mathbf{z}_{\text{ini},m}(k)$ which is an input to the predictor).
\end{proof}

We now formulate the NTDPC problem.\vspace{3mm}

\textbf{Problem 4 [NTDPC]}:
Given past input and measured output trajectories, NTDPC solves the following optimization problem at each time step \( k \):
\begin{align}
\min_{\mathbf{u}_N(k), \mathbf{y}_N(k),\sigma_y(k)} & \quad \|\mathbf{y}_N(k)\|_Q^2 + \|\mathbf{u}_N(k)\|_R^2  + \|\sigma_y(k)\|_{\Lambda_y}^2 \notag \\
\text{s.t.} \quad & \hat{\mathbf{y}}_N(k) = \mathbf{P}_1 \mathbf{z}_{\text{ini},m}(k)  + \mathbf{P}_2 \mathbf{u}_N(k) + \sigma_y(k), \label{eq:NTDPC_constraint} \\
& \mathbf{u}_N(k) \in \mathcal{U}, \quad \hat{\mathbf{y}}_N(k) \in \mathcal{Y}, \notag
\end{align}
where \(\mathbf{u}_N(k) \in \mathbb{R}^{n_u N}\) is the input sequence over horizon \( N \), \(\mathbf{y}_N(k) \in \mathbb{R}^{n_y N}\) is the predicted output sequence in the cost, \(\hat{\mathbf{y}}_N(k) \in \mathbb{R}^{n_y N}\) is the constrained output sequence (related to $\mathbf{y}_N(k)$ through the model), \(\sigma_y(k) \in \mathbb{R}^{n_y N}\) is a slack variable, \(\mathbf{z}_{\text{ini},m}(k) = \text{col}(\mathbf{u}_{\text{ini}}(k), \mathbf{y}_{\text{ini},m}(k)) \in \mathbb{R}^{(n_u + n_y)T_{\text{ini}}}\) contains past inputs and noisy outputs (\(\mathbf{y}_{\text{ini},m}(k) = \mathbf{y}_{\text{ini}}(k) + \mathbf{\zeta}_{\text{ini}}(k)\), \(\mathbf{\zeta}_{\text{ini}}(k) \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \mathbf{\Sigma}_{\zeta})\)), \(\mathbf{P}_1, \mathbf{P}_2\) are SVD-derived predictor matrices, \( Q, R, \Lambda_y \succ 0 \) are weighting matrices, and \(\mathcal{U}, \mathcal{Y}\) are convex constraint sets. Algorithm 1 summarizes the main steps of the NTDPC approach.

The offline computation of \(\mathbf{P}_1, \mathbf{P}_2\) reduces online complexity, and the absence of regularization parameters simplifies tuning compared to DeePC, \(\gamma\)-DDPC, and GDPC.

\begin{remark}
When calculating the SVD factorization for matrices $\mathbf{Z}_p$ and $\mathbf{Z}_f V_2$ in \eqref{eq:Zp_SVD_L1V1T} and \eqref{eq:svd_ZfV2} in noisy case, it is recommended to normalize the rows of these matrices first, to avoid giving higher priority to input or output. For this purpose, the input and output signals need to be scaled at the first step as $
\mathbf{u}(k)\leftarrow M_u^{-1}\mathbf{u}(k), 
\mathbf{y}(k)\leftarrow M_y^{-1}\mathbf{y}(k)$
where $M_u=\text{diag}(m_{u_1},\cdots,m_{u_{n_u}})$ and 
$M_y=\text{diag} (m_{y_1},\cdots,m_{y_{n_y}})$ in which $m_{u_i}$ and $m_{y_i}$ shows the range of $\mathbf{u}_i$ and $\mathbf{y}_i$, respectively. In this study,  
$
m_{u_i}=\text{mean}(|\mathbf{u}_i|);\quad m_{y_i}=\text{mean}(|\mathbf{y}_i|)$.
At the end, the calculated control input $\mathbf{u}(k)$ needs to be denormalized before applying to the plant.
\end{remark}
\vspace{3mm}

\begin{algorithm}
\caption{Implementation of NTDPC}
\label{alg:dp_constrained_consensus}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\REQUIRE Generate P.E input and measure output 
\STATE Normalize the input and output signals 
\STATE Construct $\mathbf{U}_p, \mathbf{Y}_p, \mathbf{U}_f, \mathbf{Y}_f$ 
\STATE Compute and extract $L_1,V_1,V_2,L_{f_1}$ using SVD
\STATE Calculate the matrices $\mathbf{P}_1,\mathbf{P}_2$
\STATE Build $\mathbf{z}_{\text{ini},m}(k)$  using the past $T_{\text{ini}}$ data samples
\STATE Obtain $\hat{\mathbf{y}}_N (k)= \mathbf{P}_1~{\mathbf{z}}_{\text{ini},m}(k) + \mathbf{P}_2~\mathbf{u}_N(k)$
\STATE Solve the QP problem (Problem 4) and obtain $\mathbf{u}_N^*(k)$
\STATE Denormalize the signal $\mathbf{u}_N^*(k)$
\STATE Apply $u(k) = \mathbf{u}_{N_{[0:n_u-1]}}^*(k)$ to the system  
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm} 
\vspace{-3mm}

\section{Stability Analysis}
This section establishes a rigorous stability analysis of the developed NTDPC approach. 
For both noise-free and noisy cases, we establish stability to the desired equilibrium. In the noise-free case, asymptotic stability ensures tracking of the reference. In the noisy case, input-to-state stability (ISS) guarantees robustness to bounded measurement noise. To facilitate the stability analysis, we define key concepts and notations as done in \cite{lazar2021dissipativity} for DeePC.


\begin{definition}[Stage Cost]\label{def:stage_cost}
Assume \( Q, R \) are block-diagonal:
\begin{align}
\|\mathbf{y}_N(k)\|_Q^2 &= \sum_{i=0}^{N-1} \|y^*(i|k) - r_y\|_{Q_i}^2, \notag \\
\|\mathbf{u}_N(k)\|_R^2 &= \sum_{i=0}^{N-1} \|u^*(i|k) - r_u\|_{R_i}^2,
\end{align}
where \( Q_i, R_i \succ 0 \), and \( r_y \in \mathbb{R}^{n_y} \), \( r_u \in \mathbb{R}^{n_u} \) are the steady-state output and input, while \( y^*(i|k), u^*(i|k) \) are the predicted output and input at step \( i \) from time \( k \). The stage cost is:
\begin{align*}
l(y^*(i|k), u^*(i|k)) &= \|y^*(i|k) - r_y\|_{Q_i}^2  + \|u^*(i|k) - r_u\|_{R_i}^2.
\end{align*}
Thus, \( J^*(k) = \sum_{i=0}^{N-1} l(y^*(i|k), u^*(i|k))  + \|\sigma_y^*(k)\|_{\Lambda_y}^2 \).
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Non-Minimal State]\label{def:non_minimal_state}
The non-minimal state is:
\begin{align*}
X_{\text{ini}}(k) &= \mathbf{z}_{\text{ini}}(k) = \text{col}(\mathbf{y}_{\text{ini}}(k), \mathbf{u}_{\text{ini}}(k)) = \text{col}(y(k-T_{\text{ini}}), \dots, y(k-1), u(k-T_{\text{ini}}), \dots, u(k-1)),
\end{align*}
with \(\mathbf{y}_{\text{ini}}(k) \in \mathbb{R}^{n_y T_{\text{ini}}}, \mathbf{u}_{\text{ini}}(k) \in \mathbb{R}^{n_u T_{\text{ini}}}\). The error state is:
\begin{align*}
\tilde{X}_{\text{ini}}(k) &= \text{col}(\mathbf{y}_{\text{ini}}(k) - \mathbf{r}_y, \mathbf{u}_{\text{ini}}(k) - \mathbf{r}_u),
\end{align*}
where \(\mathbf{r}_y = \text{col}(r_y, \dots, r_y)\), \(\mathbf{r}_u = \text{col}(r_u, \dots, r_u)\).
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Class \(\mathcal{K}\) and \(\mathcal{KL}\) Functions]\label{def:class_k_kl}
A function \(\varphi : \mathbb{R}_+ \to \mathbb{R}_+\) is of class \(\mathcal{K}\) if it is continuous, strictly increasing, and \(\varphi(0) = 0\). It is \(\mathcal{K}_{\infty}\) if \(\lim_{s \to \infty} \varphi(s) = \infty\). A function \(\beta : \mathbb{R}_+ \times \mathbb{R}_+ \to \mathbb{R}_+\) is \(\mathcal{KL}\) if \(\beta(\cdot, t) \in \mathcal{K}\) for fixed \( t \geq 0 \), and \(\beta(s, t) \to 0\) as \( t \to \infty \) for fixed \( s > 0 \). The identity is \( \text{id}(s) = s \).
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Filtration]\label{def:filtration}
The filtration \(\mathcal{F}_k\) is the sigma-algebra generated by the measurements \(\{\mathbf{z}_{\text{ini},m}(t)\}_{t=0}^k\), representing the information available up to time \( k \).
\end{definition}

\subsection{Noise-Free Case}
We analyze the stability of NTDPC in the noise-free case (\(\mathbf{\zeta}_{\text{ini}}(k) = 0\)), tracking references \((r_y, r_u)\).

\begin{assumption}[Positive Definiteness]\label{ass:positive_definiteness_cost}
There exist \(\alpha_1, \alpha_2 \in \mathcal{K}_{\infty}\) such that for all \((y, u) \in \mathcal{Y}_i \times \mathcal{U}_i\), where \(\mathcal{Y}_i \subseteq \mathbb{R}^{n_y}\) and \(\mathcal{U}_i \subseteq \mathbb{R}^{n_u}\) are the projections of \(\mathcal{Y}\) and \(\mathcal{U}\) onto the \(i\)-th prediction step:
\[
\alpha_1(\|\text{col}(y - r_y, u - r_u)\|) \leq l(y, u) \leq \alpha_2(\|\text{col}(y - r_y, u - r_u)\|),
\]
where \(l(y, u) = \|y - r_y\|_{Q_i}^2 + \|u - r_u\|_{R_i}^2\), and \((r_y, r_u) \in \mathcal{Y}_i \times \mathcal{U}_i\) is a constant admissible equilibrium.
\end{assumption}

\begin{assumption}[Terminal Stabilizing Condition]\label{ass:terminal_condition_nf}
For any admissible initial state \(\mathbf{z}_{\text{ini}}(k) \in \mathcal{U}^{T_{\text{ini}}} \times \mathcal{Y}^{T_{\text{ini}}}\), there exist \(\rho \in \mathcal{K}_{\infty}\) with \(\rho < \text{id}\), a prediction horizon \(N \geq T_{\text{ini}}\), and \(\hat{u}(N|k) \in \mathcal{U}_N \subseteq \mathbb{R}^{n_u}\) such that \(\hat{y}(N|k) \in \mathcal{Y}_N \subseteq \mathbb{R}^{n_y}\):
\[
l(\hat{y}(N|k), \hat{u}(N|k)) \leq (\text{id} - \rho) \circ l(y(k - T_{\text{ini}}), u(k - T_{\text{ini}})).
\]
\end{assumption}

\begin{assumption}[Feasibility]\label{ass:feasibility_nf}
Problem 4 is feasible for all \(k \geq T_{\text{ini}}\) with \(\mathbf{\zeta}_{\text{ini}}(k) = 0\), and the optimal slack satisfies \(\sigma_y^*(k) = 0\). The sets \(\mathcal{U}, \mathcal{Y}\) contain the equilibrium \((r_y, r_u)\).
\end{assumption}

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:stability_nf}
Under Assumptions \ref{ass:positive_definiteness_cost}, \ref{ass:terminal_condition_nf}, and \ref{ass:feasibility_nf}, with \(\mathbf{\zeta}_{\text{ini}}(k) = 0\) and a constant admissible equilibrium \((r_y, r_u)\), the closed-loop system under NTDPC is asymptotically stable, i.e., \(y(k) \to r_y\), \(u(k) \to r_u\) as \(k \to \infty\).
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Define the non-minimal state as in Definition \ref{def:non_minimal_state}:
\[
\mathbf{z}_{\text{ini}}(k) = \text{col}(y(k - T_{\text{ini}}), \ldots, y(k - 1), u(k - T_{\text{ini}}), \ldots, u(k - 1)).
\]
The error state is $\tilde{X}_{\text{ini}}(k) = \mathbf{z}_{\text{ini}}(k) - \text{col}(\mathbf{r}_y, \mathbf{r}_u),$
where \(\mathbf{r}_y = \text{col}(r_y, \ldots, r_y)\), \(\mathbf{r}_u = \text{col}(r_u, \ldots, r_u)\). Define the auxiliary function as 
$W(\mathbf{z}_{\text{ini}}(k)) = \sum_{i=1}^{T_{\text{ini}}} l(y(k - i), u(k - i)),$
where the stage cost is $l(y(i|k), u(i|k)) = \|y(i|k) - r_y\|_{Q_i}^2 + \|u(i|k) - r_u\|_{R_i}^2,
$ with \(Q_i, R_i \succ 0\). The optimal cost of Problem 4 is $ J^*(k) = \sum_{i=0}^{N-1} l(y^*(i|k), u^*(i|k)),
$ since \(\sigma_y^*(k) = 0\) in the noise-free case (Assumption \ref{ass:feasibility_nf}). Consider the storage function \cite{lazar2021dissipativity}:
\[
V(\mathbf{z}_{\text{ini}}(k)) = J^*(k) + W(\mathbf{z}_{\text{ini}}(k)).
\]
To show positive definiteness of \(V(\mathbf{z}_{\text{ini}}(k))\), 
using Assumption \ref{ass:positive_definiteness_cost}, we have:
\begin{align*}
   l(y(k - i), u(k - i)) \geq \alpha_1(\|\text{col}(y(k - i) - r_y, u(k - i) - r_u)\|), 
\end{align*}
so $W(\mathbf{z}_{\text{ini}}(k)) \geq \sum_{i=1}^{T_{\text{ini}}} \alpha_1(\|\text{col}(y(k - i) - r_y, u(k - i) - r_u)\|).$ 
Since \(\tilde{X}_{\text{ini}}(k) = \text{col}(y(k - T_{\text{ini}}) - r_y, \ldots, u(k - 1) - r_u)\), there exists \(\alpha_{1,W} \in \mathcal{K}_{\infty}\) such that $
W(\mathbf{z}_{\text{ini}}(k)) \geq \alpha_{1,W}(\|\tilde{X}_{\text{ini}}(k)\|).$
Since \(J^*(k) \geq 0\), there exists \(\alpha_{1,V} \in \mathcal{K}_{\infty}\) such that $V(\mathbf{z}_{\text{ini}}(k)) \geq \alpha_{1,V}(\|\tilde{X}_{\text{ini}}(k)\|).$
For the upper bound:
\[
l(y(k - i), u(k - i)) \leq \alpha_2(\|\text{col}(y(k - i) - r_y, u(k - i) - r_u)\|),
\]
so $W(\mathbf{z}_{\text{ini}}(k)) \leq T_{\text{ini}} \alpha_2\left( \max_{i=1,\dots,T_{\text{ini}}} \|\text{col}(y(k - i) - r_y, u(k - i) - r_u)\| \right).$
 For \(J^*(k)\), the predictor output \(\hat{\mathbf{y}}_N^*(k) = \mathbf{P}_1 \mathbf{z}_{\text{ini}}(k) + \mathbf{P}_2 \mathbf{u}_N^*(k)\) (Lemma \ref{lem:predictor_unbiased}) depends on \(\mathbf{z}_{\text{ini}}(k) = \tilde{X}_{\text{ini}}(k) + \text{col}(\mathbf{r}_y, \mathbf{r}_u)\). Bounded sets \(\mathcal{Y}, ~ \mathcal{U}\) (Assumption \ref{ass:feasibility_nf}) and the predictor’s linearity ensure that \(\|y^*(i|k) - r_y\|\), \(\|u^*(i|k) - r_u\|\) are bounded by \(\|\tilde{X}_{\text{ini}}(k)\|\). Thus, there exists \(\alpha_{2,J} \in \mathcal{K}_{\infty}\) such that $J^*(k) \leq \alpha_{2,J}(\|\tilde{X}_{\text{ini}}(k)\|).$
Combining, there exists \(\alpha_{2,V} \in \mathcal{K}_{\infty}\) such that $V(\mathbf{z}_{\text{ini}}(k)) \leq \alpha_{2,V}(\|\tilde{X}_{\text{ini}}(k)\|).$
Hence, $\alpha_{1,V}(\|\tilde{X}_{\text{ini}}(k)\|) \leq V(\mathbf{z}_{\text{ini}}(k)) \leq \alpha_{2,V}(\|\tilde{X}_{\text{ini}}(k)\|).$ 
Next, by defining the supply function as:
\[
s(y(k - T_{\text{ini}}), u(k - T_{\text{ini}})) = l(\hat{y}(N|k), \hat{u}(N|k)) - l(y(k - T_{\text{ini}}), u(k - T_{\text{ini}})),
\]
where \(\hat{y}(N|k)\), \(\hat{u}(N|k)\) satisfy Assumption \ref{ass:terminal_condition_nf}. Construct a suboptimal solution at \(k+1\):
\begin{align}
  \mathbf{u}_s(k+1) &= \text{col}(u^*(1|k), \ldots, u^*(N-1|k), \hat{u}(N|k)) \notag \\  \mathbf{y}_s(k+1) &= \text{col}(y^*(1|k), \ldots, y^*(N-1|k), \hat{y}(N|k)), 
  \label{eqyus}
\end{align}
with \(\sigma_{y,s}(k+1) = 0\). The suboptimal cost is as $J_s(k+1) = \sum_{j=1}^{N-1} l(y^*(j|k), u^*(j|k)) + l(\hat{y}(N|k), \hat{u}(N|k)).$
Since \(J^*(k) = \sum_{j=0}^{N-1} l(y^*(j|k), u^*(j|k))\) and \(J^*(k+1) \leq J_s(k+1)\):
\begin{align}
   J^*(k+1) \leq J^*(k) - l(y^*(0|k), u^*(0|k)) + l(\hat{y}(N|k), \hat{u}(N|k)) 
   \label{eq:tt1}
\end{align}
For the auxiliary function \(W\):
\begin{align}
  W(\mathbf{z}_{\text{ini}}(k+1)) - W(\mathbf{z}_{\text{ini}}(k)) = l(y^*(0|k), u^*(0|k)) - l(y(k - T_{\text{ini}}), u(k - T_{\text{ini}}))
  \label{eq:tt2}
\end{align}
Using \eqref{eq:tt1} and \eqref{eq:tt2}, thus:
\begin{align}
   V(\mathbf{z}_{\text{ini}}(k+1)) - V(\mathbf{z}_{\text{ini}}(k)) = [J^*(k+1) - J^*(k)] + [W(\mathbf{z}_{\text{ini}}(k+1)) - W(\mathbf{z}_{\text{ini}}(k))]
   \label{eq:tt3}
\end{align}
Then, we can rewrite the preceding equality in \eqref{eq:tt3} as:
\begin{align}
   V(\mathbf{z}_{\text{ini}}(k+1)) - V(\mathbf{z}_{\text{ini}}(k)) &\leq [J^*(k) - l(y^*(0|k), u^*(0|k)) + l(\hat{y}(N|k), \hat{u}(N|k))] \notag \\& \quad - J^*(k) + [l(y^*(0|k), u^*(0|k)) - l(y(k - T_{\text{ini}}), u(k - T_{\text{ini}}))]\notag \\& = s(y(k - T_{\text{ini}}), u(k - T_{\text{ini}})) 
   \label{eq:y1}
\end{align}
By Assumption \ref{ass:terminal_condition_nf}, $s(y(k - T_{\text{ini}}), u(k - T_{\text{ini}})) \leq -\rho \circ l(y(k - T_{\text{ini}}), u(k - T_{\text{ini}})).
$ Since \(l(y(k - T_{\text{ini}}), u(k - T_{\text{ini}})) \geq \alpha_1(\|\text{col}(y(k - T_{\text{ini}}) - r_y, u(k - T_{\text{ini}}) - r_u)\|)\), we can rewrite the inequality \eqref{eq:y1} as:
\[
V(\mathbf{z}_{\text{ini}}(k+1)) - V(\mathbf{z}_{\text{ini}}(k)) \leq -\rho \circ \alpha_1(\|\text{col}(y(k - T_{\text{ini}}) - r_y, u(k - T_{\text{ini}}) - r_u)\|) \leq -\alpha_{1,V}(\|\tilde{X}_{\text{ini}}(k)\|).
\]
Since \(V\) is positive definite and radially unbounded with respect to \(\tilde{X}_{\text{ini}}(k)\), and satisfies a strict dissipation inequality, standard Lyapunov results \cite{jiang2001input} imply \(\tilde{X}_{\text{ini}}(k) \to 0\) as \(k \to \infty\). Thus, \(y(k) \to r_y\), \(u(k) \to r_u\), proving asymptotic stability.
Since \(V(\mathbf{z}_{\text{ini}}(k)) \leq \alpha_{2,V}(\|\tilde{X}_{\text{ini}}(k)\|)\) and \(W(\mathbf{z}_{\text{ini}}(k)) \geq 0\):
\[
J^*(k) \leq V(\mathbf{z}_{\text{ini}}(k)) \leq \alpha_{2,V}(\|\tilde{X}_{\text{ini}}(k)\|),
\]
ensuring the cost is bounded by a \(\mathcal{K}_{\infty}\) function of the error state. This completes the proof. 
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
The bound \(J^*(k) \leq \alpha_{2,V}(\|\tilde{X}_{\text{ini}}(k)\|)\) satisfies the requirement for a \(\mathcal{K}_{\infty}\) cost bound, derived within the storage function analysis.
\end{remark}

\subsection{Noisy Case: Robustness to Measurement Noise}
We analyze the stability of NTDPC under measurement noise (\(\mathbf{\zeta}_{\text{ini}}(k) \neq 0\)), tracking constant admissible references \((r_y, r_u)\). The system is shown to be input-to-state stable (ISS) with respect to \(\mathbf{\zeta}_{\text{ini}}(k)\), following the framework in \cite{verheijen2023handbook, jiang2001input}.

\begin{assumption}[Bounded Noise Variance]\label{ass:bounded_noise}
There exists \(\bar{c} > 0\) such that \(\mathbb{E}[\|\mathbf{\zeta}_{\text{ini}}(k)\|^2 | \mathcal{F}_k] \leq \bar{c}\) for all \(k\), where \(\mathcal{F}_k\) is the filtration up to time \(k\).
\end{assumption}

\begin{assumption}[Lipschitz Predictor]\label{ass:lipschitz_predictor}
There exists \(L_P > 0\) such that:
\[
\|\mathbf{P}_1 (\mathbf{z}_{\text{ini},m}(k) - \mathbf{z}_{\text{ini}}(k))\| \leq L_P \|\mathbf{\zeta}_{\text{ini}}(k)\|,
\]
where \(L_P = \|\mathbf{P}_1\|_2\) is the spectral norm of \(\mathbf{P}_1\), and \(\mathbf{z}_{\text{ini},m}(k) = \mathbf{z}_{\text{ini}}(k) + \text{col}(0, \mathbf{\zeta}_{\text{ini}}(k))\).
\end{assumption}

\begin{assumption}[Terminal Stabilizing Condition - Noisy]\label{ass:terminal_condition_noisy}
For any admissible initial state \(\mathbf{z}_{\text{ini}}(k) \in \mathcal{U}^{T_{\text{ini}}} \times \mathcal{Y}^{T_{\text{ini}}}\), there exist \(\rho \in \mathcal{K}_{\infty}\) with \(\rho < id\), a prediction horizon \(N \geq T_{\text{ini}}\), and \(\hat{u}(N|k) \in \mathcal{U}_N \subseteq \mathbb{R}^{n_u}\) such that \(\hat{y}(N|k) \in \mathcal{Y}_N \subseteq \mathbb{R}^{n_y}\):
\[
\mathbb{E}[l(\hat{y}(N|k), \hat{u}(N|k)) | \mathcal{F}_k] \leq (\text{id} - \rho) \circ \mathbb{E}[l(y(k - T_{\text{ini}}), u(k - T_{\text{ini}})) | \mathcal{F}_k].
\]
\end{assumption}

\begin{assumption}[Feasibility - Noisy]\label{ass:feasibility_noisy}
Problem 4 is feasible with probability at least \(1 - \epsilon\), for small \(\epsilon > 0\). There exists \(c_\sigma > 0\) such that:
\[
\mathbb{E}[\|\sigma_y^*(k)\|_{\Lambda_y}^2 | \mathcal{F}_k] \leq c_\sigma \mathbb{E}[\|\mathbf{\zeta}_{\text{ini}}(k)\|^2 | \mathcal{F}_k].
\]
The sets \(\mathcal{U}, \mathcal{Y}\) contain the equilibrium \((r_y, r_u)\).
\end{assumption}

\begin{remark}
Assumption \ref{ass:terminal_condition_noisy} is satisfied by selecting \(\hat{u}(N|k) = r_u\) and leveraging the predictor’s robustness (Lemma \ref{lem:predictor_unbiased}). Assumption \ref{ass:feasibility_noisy} is supported by the sensitivity index \(I_s \leq 0.7\) (Fig.\ \ref{fig:ISF}), ensuring noise separability and feasibility.
\end{remark}

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:stability_noisy}
Under Assumptions \ref{ass:positive_definiteness_cost}, \ref{ass:bounded_noise}, \ref{ass:lipschitz_predictor}, \ref{ass:terminal_condition_noisy}, and \ref{ass:feasibility_noisy}, with a constant admissible equilibrium \((r_y, r_u)\), NTDPC is input-to-state stable (ISS) with respect to \(\mathbf{\zeta}_{\text{ini}}(k)\). There exist \(\beta \in \mathcal{KL}\), \(\gamma \in \mathcal{K}_{\infty}\) such that:
\[
\mathbb{E}[\|\tilde{X}_{\text{ini}}(k)\|] \leq \beta(\|\tilde{X}_{\text{ini}}(0)\|, k) + \gamma\left(\sup_{0 \leq t < k} \|\mathbf{\zeta}_{\text{ini}}(t)\|_{\infty}\right).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Define the non-minimal state as in Definition \ref{def:non_minimal_state}:
\[
\mathbf{z}_{\text{ini}}(k) = \text{col}(y(k - T_{\text{ini}}), \ldots, y(k - 1), u(k - T_{\text{ini}}), \ldots, u(k - 1)),
\]
and the error state $\tilde{X}_{\text{ini}}(k) = \mathbf{z}_{\text{ini}}(k) - \text{col}(\mathbf{r}_y, \mathbf{r}_u),$
where \(\mathbf{r}_y = \text{col}(r_y, \ldots, r_y)\), \(\mathbf{r}_u = \text{col}(r_u, \ldots, r_u)\). The auxiliary function is $W(\mathbf{z}_{\text{ini}}(k)) = \sum_{i=1}^{T_{\text{ini}}} l(y(k - i), u(k - i)),$ with stage cost $l(y(i|k), u(i|k)) = \|y(i|k) - r_y\|_{Q_i}^2 + \|u(i|k) - r_u\|_{R_i}^2,$
and \(Q_i, R_i \succ 0\). The optimal cost of Problem 4 is $J^*(k) = \sum_{i=0}^{N-1} l(y^*(i|k), u^*(i|k)) + \|\sigma_y^*(k)\|_{\Lambda_y}^2.$
Define the stochastic Lyapunov function:
\[
V(k) = \mathbb{E}[J^*(k) + W(\mathbf{z}_{\text{ini}}(k)) | \mathcal{F}_k].
\]
In order to study positive definiteness of $V(k)$, under Assumption \ref{ass:positive_definiteness_cost}, we have $l(y(k - i), u(k - i)) \geq \alpha_1(\|\text{col}(y(k - i) - r_y, u(k - i) - r_u)\|),$ so $W(\mathbf{z}_{\text{ini}}(k)) \geq \sum_{i=1}^{T_{\text{ini}}} \alpha_1(\|\text{col}(y(k - i) - r_y, u(k - i) - r_u)\|) \geq \alpha_{1,W}(\|\tilde{X}_{\text{ini}}(k)\|),$
for some \(\alpha_{1,W} \in \mathcal{K}_{\infty}\). For \(J^*(k)\), the predictor output \(\hat{\mathbf{y}}_N^*(k) = \mathbf{P}_1 \mathbf{z}_{\text{ini},m}(k) + \mathbf{P}_2 \mathbf{u}_N^*(k) + \sigma_y^*(k)\) and bounded sets \(\mathcal{Y}, \mathcal{U}\) (Assumption \ref{ass:feasibility_noisy}) imply that \(\|y^*(i|k) - r_y\|\), \(\|u^*(i|k) - r_u\|\) are bounded by \(\|\tilde{X}_{\text{ini}}(k)\|\) and noise effects. By Assumption \ref{ass:feasibility_noisy}:
\[
\mathbb{E}[\|\sigma_y^*(k)\|_{\Lambda_y}^2 | \mathcal{F}_k] \leq c_\sigma \mathbb{E}[\|\mathbf{\zeta}_{\text{ini}}(k)\|^2 | \mathcal{F}_k] \leq c_\sigma \bar{c}.
\]
Thus, there exists \(\alpha_{2,J} \in \mathcal{K}_{\infty}\) such that:
\[
\mathbb{E}[J^*(k) | \mathcal{F}_k] \leq \alpha_{2,J}(\|\tilde{X}_{\text{ini}}(k)\|) + c_\sigma \bar{c}.
\]
Combining, there exist \(\alpha_{1,V}, \alpha_{2,V} \in \mathcal{K}_{\infty}\) such that:
\[
\alpha_{1,V}(\|\tilde{X}_{\text{ini}}(k)\|) \leq V(k) \leq \alpha_{2,V}(\|\tilde{X}_{\text{ini}}(k)\|) + c_\sigma \bar{c}.
\]
As dissipation inequality, we 
construct a suboptimal solution at \(k+1\) like \eqref{eqyus} with \(\sigma_{y,s}(k+1) = \mathbf{P}_1 \mathbf{z}_{\text{ini},m}(k+1) + \mathbf{P}_2 \mathbf{u}_s(k+1) - \mathbf{y}_s(k+1)\), where \(\hat{u}(N|k), \hat{y}(N|k)\) satisfy Assumption \ref{ass:terminal_condition_noisy}. The suboptimal cost is:
\[
J_s(k+1) = \sum_{j=1}^{N-1} l(y^*(j|k), u^*(j|k)) + l(\hat{y}(N|k), \hat{u}(N|k)) + \|\sigma_{y,s}(k+1)\|_{\Lambda_y}^2.
\]
By computing $\mathbb{E}[J_s(k+1) | \mathcal{F}_k]$ as follows:
\begin{align}
  \mathbb{E}[J_s(k+1) | \mathcal{F}_k] = \mathbb{E}\left[ \sum_{j=1}^{N-1} l(y^*(j|k), u^*(j|k)) + l(\hat{y}(N|k), \hat{u}(N|k)) + \|\sigma_{y,s}(k+1)\|_{\Lambda_y}^2 \Big| \mathcal{F}_k \right]
  \label{eq:t1}
\end{align}
Since:
\begin{align}
   \mathbb{E}[J^*(k) | \mathcal{F}_k] = \mathbb{E}\left[ \sum_{j=0}^{N-1} l(y^*(j|k), u^*(j|k)) + \|\sigma_y^*(k)\|_{\Lambda_y}^2 \Big| \mathcal{F}_k \right]
   \label{eq:t2}
\end{align}
Then, by subtracting \eqref{eq:t1} and \eqref{eq:t2}, we have:
\begin{align*}
 \mathbb{E}[J_s(k+1) - J^*(k) | \mathcal{F}_k] &= -\mathbb{E}[l(y^*(0|k), u^*(0|k)) | \mathcal{F}_k] - \mathbb{E}[\|\sigma_y^*(k)\|_{\Lambda_y}^2 | \mathcal{F}_k] \\& \quad + \mathbb{E}[l(\hat{y}(N|k), \hat{u}(N|k)) | \mathcal{F}_k] + \mathbb{E}[\|\sigma_{y,s}(k+1)\|_{\Lambda_y}^2 | \mathcal{F}_k]   
\end{align*}
By Assumption \ref{ass:terminal_condition_noisy}:
\begin{align}
  \mathbb{E}[l(\hat{y}(N|k), \hat{u}(N|k)) | \mathcal{F}_k] \leq (id - \rho) \circ \mathbb{E}[l(y(k - T_{\text{ini}}), u(k - T_{\text{ini}})) | \mathcal{F}_k].
  \label{eq:w0}
\end{align}
For the slack terms, by Assumption \ref{ass:lipschitz_predictor}:
\begin{align}
  \|\sigma_{y,s}(k+1)\| \leq \|\mathbf{P}_1 (\mathbf{z}_{\text{ini},m}(k+1) - \mathbf{z}_{\text{ini}}(k+1))\| \leq L_P \|\mathbf{\zeta}_{\text{ini}}(k+1)\| 
  \label{eq:tt1}
\end{align}
so, by taking the expectation from both side of \eqref{eq:tt1}:
\[
\mathbb{E}[\|\sigma_{y,s}(k+1)\|_{\Lambda_y}^2 | \mathcal{F}_k] \leq \lambda_{\max}(\Lambda_y) L_P^2 \mathbb{E}[\|\mathbf{\zeta}_{\text{ini}}(k+1)\|^2 | \mathcal{F}_k] \leq \lambda_{\max}(\Lambda_y) L_P^2 \bar{c}.
\]
Similarly, \(\mathbb{E}[\|\sigma_y^*(k)\|_{\Lambda_y}^2 | \mathcal{F}_k] \leq c_\sigma \bar{c}\). Let \(c_{\sigma,\text{max}} = \max(c_\sigma, \lambda_{\max}(\Lambda_y) L_P^2)\). Then:
\begin{align}
\mathbb{E}[J_s(k+1) - J^*(k) | \mathcal{F}_k] \leq -\rho \circ \mathbb{E}[l(y(k - T_{\text{ini}}), u(k - T_{\text{ini}})) | \mathcal{F}_k] + 2 c_{\sigma,\text{max}} \bar{c}.
 \label{eq:w1}
\end{align}
And for \(W\):
\begin{align}
 W(\mathbf{z}_{\text{ini}}(k+1)) - W(\mathbf{z}_{\text{ini}}(k)) = l(y^*(0|k), u^*(0|k)) - l(y(k - T_{\text{ini}}), u(k - T_{\text{ini}})) 
 \label{eq:w2}
\end{align}
By taking the expectation from both side of \eqref{eq:w2}, thus:
\begin{align}
\mathbb{E}[W(\mathbf{z}_{\text{ini}}(k+1)) - W(\mathbf{z}_{\text{ini}}(k)) | \mathcal{F}_k] = \mathbb{E}[l(y^*(0|k), u^*(0|k)) - l(y(k - T_{\text{ini}}), u(k - T_{\text{ini}})) | \mathcal{F}_k].
 \label{eq:w3}
\end{align}
By combining \eqref{eq:w1} and \eqref{eq:w3}:
\begin{align}
  V(k+1) - V(k) \leq \mathbb{E}[J_s(k+1) - J^*(k) + W(\mathbf{z}_{\text{ini}}(k+1)) - W(\mathbf{z}_{\text{ini}}(k)) | \mathcal{F}_k].
  \label{eq:R1}
\end{align}
By substituting \eqref{eq:w0} in \eqref{eq:R1}, yields:
\[
V(k+1) - V(k) \leq -\rho \circ \mathbb{E}[l(y(k - T_{\text{ini}}), u(k - T_{\text{ini}})) | \mathcal{F}_k] + \mathbb{E}[l(y^*(0|k), u^*(0|k)) | \mathcal{F}_k] + 2 c_{\sigma,\text{max}} \bar{c}.
\]
By Assumption \ref{ass:positive_definiteness_cost}, there exists \(\alpha_4 \in \mathcal{K}_{\infty}\) such that:
\begin{align}
  \mathbb{E}[l(y(k - T_{\text{ini}}), u(k - T_{\text{ini}})) | \mathcal{F}_k] \geq \alpha_4(\mathbb{E}[\|\text{col}(y(k - T_{\text{ini}}) - r_y, u(k - T_{\text{ini}}) - r_u)\|])
  \label{eq:w32}
\end{align}
Since \(\tilde{X}_{\text{ini}}(k)\) includes these terms and using \eqref{eq:w32}, there exists \(\alpha_{4,V} \in \mathcal{K}_{\infty}\) such that:
\[
V(k+1) - V(k) \leq -\rho \circ \alpha_{4,V}(\mathbb{E}[\|\tilde{X}_{\text{ini}}(k)\|]) + \mathbb{E}[l(y^*(0|k), u^*(0|k)) | \mathcal{F}_k] + 2 c_{\sigma,\text{max}} \bar{c}.
\]
The term \(\mathbb{E}[l(y^*(0|k), u^*(0|k)) | \mathcal{F}_k]\) is bounded by the predictor’s output and noise effects, contributing to the ISS gain.

To show ISS property, 
By Assumption \ref{ass:bounded_noise}, \(\bar{c} \leq c_{\zeta} \sup_{t \leq k} \|\mathbf{\zeta}_{\text{ini}}(t)\|_{\infty}^2\), the dissipation inequality:
\[
V(k+1) \leq V(k) - \rho \circ \alpha_{4,V}(\mathbb{E}[\|\tilde{X}_{\text{ini}}(k)\|]) + \alpha_5(\mathbb{E}[\|\tilde{X}_{\text{ini}}(k)\|]) + c_{\zeta,\text{max}} \sup_{t \leq k} \|\mathbf{\zeta}_{\text{ini}}(t)\|_{\infty}^2,
\]
where \(\alpha_5 \in \mathcal{K}_{\infty}\) bounds the control cost, and \(c_{\zeta,\text{max}} = 2 c_{\sigma,\text{max}} c_{\zeta}\). Applying stochastic ISS results \cite{jiang2001input}, there exist \(\beta \in \mathcal{KL}\), \(\gamma \in \mathcal{K}_{\infty}\) such that:
\[
\mathbb{E}[\|\tilde{X}_{\text{ini}}(k)\|] \leq \beta(\|\tilde{X}_{\text{ini}}(0)\|, k) + \gamma\left(\sup_{0 \leq t < k} \|\mathbf{\zeta}_{\text{ini}}(t)\|_{\infty}\right).
\]

Since \(V(k) \leq \alpha_{2,V}(\|\tilde{X}_{\text{ini}}(k)\|) + c_\sigma \bar{c}\) and \(\mathbb{E}[W(\mathbf{z}_{\text{ini}}(k)) | \mathcal{F}_k] \geq 0\):
\[
\mathbb{E}[J^*(k) | \mathcal{F}_k] \leq \alpha_{2,V}(\|\tilde{X}_{\text{ini}}(k)\|) + c_\sigma \bar{c},
\]
ensuring the cost is bounded by a \(\mathcal{K}_{\infty}\) function of the error state plus a noise-dependent term. Hence, the proof is completed.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
The ISS property ensures bounded tracking errors under noise, as validated in Fig.\ \ref{fig:Y1}, where NTDPC maintains stable tracking of \((r_y, r_u)\) with \(\sigma_{\zeta}^2 = 0.25I\). The sensitivity index \(I_s\) (Fig.\ \ref{fig:ISF}) supports Assumption \ref{ass:feasibility_noisy}, aiding horizon selection.
\end{remark}

\section{Numerical Simulations}

Consider the discrete-time linear model system we aim to control, characterized by the following matrices \cite{camacho2007introduction}, Section 6.4:
\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
& A =
\begin{bmatrix}
0.9997 & 0.0038 & -0.0001 & -0.0322 \\
-0.0056 & 0.9648 & 0.7446 & 0.0001 \\
0.0020 & -0.0097 & 0.9543 & -0.0000 \\
0.0001 & -0.0005 & 0.0978 & 1.0000
\end{bmatrix},
\quad\\&
B =
\begin{bmatrix}
0.0010 & 0.1000 \\
-0.0615 & 0.0183 \\
-0.1133 & 0.0586 \\
-0.0057 & 0.0029
\end{bmatrix},
C =
\begin{bmatrix}
1.0000 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\
0 & -1.0000 & 0 & 7.7400
\end{bmatrix}
\end{split}
\end{equation}
These matrices are obtained by discretizing the dynamics of the longitudinal motion of a Boeing 747 using a zero-order hold with a sampling time $T_s = 0.1 \text{ s}$. The system has two inputs: the throttle $u_1$ and the elevator angle $u_2$, and two outputs: the aircraft's longitudinal velocity and climb rate.

The control objective is to increase the longitudinal velocity ($y_1(t)$) from 0 ft/s to 10 ft/s while maintaining the climb rate ($y_2(t)$) at zero. This corresponds to a reference output $r_y = \begin{bmatrix} 10 & 0 \end{bmatrix}^T$. The inputs and outputs are constrained as follows:
\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
&U := \left\{ u \in \mathbb{R}^2 : \begin{bmatrix} -20 \\ -20 \end{bmatrix} \leq u \leq \begin{bmatrix} 20 \\ 20 \end{bmatrix} \right\},
\quad \\&
Y := \left\{ y \in \mathbb{R}^2 : \begin{bmatrix} -25 \\ -15 \end{bmatrix} \leq y \leq \begin{bmatrix} 25 \\ 15 \end{bmatrix} \right\}.
\end{split}
\end{equation}

In this simulation, we set $T_{\text{ini}} =N= 20$, data length for Hankel matrices $M = 2500$.

\subsection{Noise-free case}

The Fig.\ref{fig:Y11} illustrates the performance of NTDPC in the noise-free case ($\mathbf{\zeta}_{\text{ini}}(k) = 0$) when tracking constant admissible references ($(r_y, r_u)$), as analyzed in Section 5.1. The results are consistent with the asymptotic stability proven in Theorem \ref{thm:stability_nf}, where $y(k) \to r_y$ and $u(k) \to r_u$ as $k \to \infty$ under Assumptions \ref{ass:positive_definiteness_cost}, \ref{ass:terminal_condition_nf}, and \ref{ass:feasibility_nf}. The top plot displays the primary output $y_1(k)$ for NTDPC (blue), SPC (red), and SMMPC (green) over time, with the dashed line representing the reference $r_y$; all methods converge to $r_y$, but NTDPC exhibits the fastest transient response and minimal overshoot, reflecting the effectiveness of the data-driven predictor and terminal condition. The second plot shows a secondary output $y_2(k)$, with similar convergence behavior, where NTDPC (blue) stabilizes more quickly than SPC (red) and SMMPC (green), supporting the robustness of the NTDPC framework to initial conditions encoded in $\mathbf{z}_{\text{ini}}(k)$. The third plot presents the primary input $u_1(k)$, where NTDPC (blue) adjusts rapidly from an initial value, stabilizing near $r_u$ (dashed line) with less oscillation compared to SPC (red) and SMMPC (green), consistent with the bounded input constraints in $\mathcal{U}$. Finally, the bottom plot depicts a secondary input $u_2(k)$, showing NTDPC (blue) achieving a steady state closer to $r_u$ with reduced variability, further validating the stability properties derived from the Lyapunov function $V(\mathbf{z}_{\text{ini}}(k))$. Overall, the convergence of outputs and inputs to their respective references demonstrates the asymptotic stability of NTDPC, as predicted by the storage function analysis $V(\mathbf{z}_{\text{ini}}(k)) = J^*(k) + W(\mathbf{z}_{\text{ini}}(k))$, and the superior performance of NTDPC over SPC and SMMPC highlights the advantage of the noise-tolerant predictor and optimization framework, particularly in the absence of noise.

