\begin{abstract}
Music-to-dance generation aims to synthesize human dance motion conditioned on musical input. Despite recent progress, significant challenges remain due to the semantic gap between music and dance motion, as music offers only abstract cues, such as melody, groove, and emotion, without explicitly specifying the physical movements. Moreover, a single piece of music can produce multiple plausible dance interpretations. This one-to-many mapping demands additional guidance, as music alone provides limited information for generating diverse dance movements. The challenge is further amplified by the scarcity of paired music and dance data, which restricts the model’s ability to learn diverse dance patterns.
% TODO: simplify
% Music-to-dance generation is still an ill-posed problem despite recent progress, because one musical piece can produce multiple dances that are equally valid.
% Music encodes abstract information such as melody, groove, and emotion, but lacks the capacity to directly specify motion actions using concrete descriptors like jump, wave, or spin, which clearly articulate the physical structure of movement. This gap is further exacerbated by the limited availability of motion-captured dance data, which restricts the model’s ability to learn a wide range of movement patterns.
% In contrast, textual instructions offer a complementary modality that can describe physical actions explicitly and convey high-level semantic guidance, helping to enhance motion diversity even under data-scarce conditions.
% Music encodes abstract features such as melody, groove, and emotion, but lacks explicit physical cues about body movements, making it a challenge for models to generate diverse and stylistically coherent dance sequences. This challenge is further compounded by the limited availability of motion-captured dance data, which constraints the model’s capacity to learn diverse movements. In contrast, text can directly describe physical actions and introduce additional semantic cues, helping to enhance motion diversity even under data-scarce conditions.
% Unlike text, which can directly describe physical motion, music lacks explicit information about body movements, making it difficult to achieve both musical alignment and stylistic diversity in generated dance sequences.
In this paper, we introduce \textbf{DanceChat}, a Large Language Model (LLM)-guided music-to-dance generation approach. 
% We treat the model as a dance learner, and utilize an LLM as a choreographer to guide it with semantically interpretable motion instructions in natural language. Rather than relying solely on implicit patterns in music, we explicitly inject motion-level textual instructions into the generation process, enable the model to learn to dance with more diversity and alignment with the style of the music. 
We use an LLM as a choreographer that provides textual motion instructions, offering explicit, high-level guidance for dance generation. This approach goes beyond implicit learning from music alone, enabling the model to generate dance that is both more diverse and better aligned with musical styles.
Our approach consists of three components: (1) an LLM-based pseudo instruction generation module that produces textual dance guidance based on music style and structure, (2) a multi-modal feature extraction and fusion module that integrates music, rhythm, and textual guidance into a shared representation, and (3) a diffusion-based motion synthesis module together with a multi-modal alignment loss, which ensures that the generated dance is aligned with both musical and textual cues. Extensive experiments on AIST++ and human evaluations show that DanceChat outperforms state-of-the-art methods both qualitatively and quantitatively.
The additional qualitative samples can be found at  \href{https://dancechat.github.io/anon/}{https://dancechat.github.io/anon/}.
\end{abstract}

