Building on these insights, our method adapts the capabilities of LLMs to address the semantic gap in music-driven dance generation. We propose a novel approach, named \emph{DanceChat}, which conceptualizes the generation model as a \textit{dancing learner} and leverages LLMs as a \textit{pseudo-choreographer}. Specifically, we extract structured musical descriptors, including key, tempo, and chord progressions alongside the dance genre context, and feed this information into LLM. The model then generates corresponding textual dance instructions, which serve as a pseudo-guide to condition the motion generation model during training. This helps reduce the semantic gap by offering interpretative cues that map abstract musical elements to concrete motion patterns.

To further enhance alignment between modalities, we encode the input music into finer-grained descriptors such as beat and downbeat positions, which are known to correlate strongly with movement timing in dance. These rhythmic cues, together with the LLM-generated textual instructions, are integrated through a multi-modal fusion module, enabling the model to form a richer and more coherent understanding of the music. By combining musical structure, rhythmic signals, and semantic instructions, DanceChat produces dance motions that are rhythmically aligned, physically plausible, and stylistically expressive.

