\subsection{Comparison to Existing Methods}

We have selected FACT \cite{li2021ai}, Bailando \cite{siyao2022bailando}, EDGE \cite{tseng2023edge}, BADM \cite{zhang2024bidirectional} and Beat-it \cite{huang2024beat} for comparisons with our DanceChat method. FACT utilizes a full-attention mechanism to capture dependencies across music and motion, leveraging autoregressive generation to maintain consistency in dance sequences. Bailando builds upon this by incorporating a VQ-VAE \cite{van2017neural} and GPT \cite{radford2018improving} framework, enhanced with an actor-critic reinforcement learning scheme to improve dance quality. EDGE introduces a transformer-based diffusion model, serving as the backbone of our method. BADM, as a follow-up to EDGE, proposes a bidirectional autoregressive diffusion model that enforces harmony in both forward and backward directions, capturing local motion details for smoother dance sequences. Similarly, Beat-it extends EDGE by presenting a beat-synchronized, key pose-guided 3D dance generation framework, integrating explicit beat awareness and key pose guidance to ensure alignment between dance motions and musical beats.

To evaluate the effectiveness of our approach, we conduct a series of experiments to assess the performance in terms of physical plausibility, motion diversity, and beat alignment score (BAS), which together evaluate the physical realism, stylistic variability, and rhythmic quality of the generated dance motion. The quantitative metrics are shown in Table \ref{tab: quantitative}. In addition to this, we also conduct a human evaluation to gather subjective feedback on the overall quality and coherence of the generated motions, as shown in Table \ref{tab:user_study}. 



\noindent\textbf{Motion Quality.} 
We incorporate the Physical Foot Contact score (PFC) \cite{tseng2023edge} to evaluate the foot-ground interactions, a critical component of physical realism in dance generation. The PFC score assesses the accuracy of foot contact behavior, ensuring that generated sequences avoid unrealistic foot sliding and maintain natural center of mass (COM) dynamics. A lower PFC score indicates that the generated movements more closely approximate realistic foot-ground interactions, with stable foot placements and controlled COM dynamics, leading to improved balance and physical plausibility. As shown in Table \ref{tab: quantitative}, DanceChat achieves the best performance in $\mathrm{PFC}$, with an improvement of 0.138 compared with SOTA method Beat-it \cite{huang2024beat}. This result highlights DanceChat’s ability to generate dance sequences with high physically realistic foot-ground interactions. 

\noindent\textbf{Motion Diversity.}
To assess the diversity of the generated dance motions, we follow the approach outlined by Bailando \cite{siyao2022bailando}. We
compute the mean Euclidean distance within the motion feature space to quantify the spread of generated motions. Specifically, we calculate two diversity metrics: $\mathrm{Div}_{k}$, which reflects diversity in the kinematic feature space, and $\mathrm{Div}_{g}$, which measures diversity in the geometric feature space. These metrics are computed over 5-second dance clips to maintain consistency with prior benchmarks \cite{tseng2023edge, zhang2024bidirectional, huang2024beat}. Following observations from EDGE \cite{tseng2023edge}, excessively jittery or unstable motions can lead to artificially inflated diversity scores, occasionally exceeding those of the ground truth. Therefore, rather than maximizing diversity, models should aim to match the distributional spread of the ground truth, indicating an appropriate balance between variation and realism. As shown in Table \ref{tab: quantitative}, our approach achieves a $\mathrm{Div}_{g}$ score of 7.36 and a $\mathrm{Div}_{k}$ score of 10.64, which are closer to the ground truth than all the other methods.
We attribute to our model’s use of global diffusion techniques and fundamental dance primitives, enabling it to learn diverse choreography patterns. 

\noindent\textbf{Beat Alignment Score (BAS).}
To evaluate the rhythmic alignment of generated dance movements with the accompanying music, we use the BAS metric, which measures the consistency between the music beats and the kinematic beats of the generated motion. Following Bailando \cite{siyao2022bailando}, this metric rewards alignment when local minima in joint speed (indicative of pauses or transitions in motion) correspond with the musical beats. 
%This metric captures the ability of the model to \textit{follow the beat} in a way that aligns naturally with the timing and rhythm of the music. 
As shown in Table \ref{tab: quantitative}, our model achieves the second-best BAS score, indicating a strong ability to follow the beat and maintain rhythmic coherence. The highest BAS is achieved by Beat-it \cite{huang2024beat}, which explicitly optimizes for beat alignment using a strong beat-supervised loss combined with key-pose constraints, allowing it to tightly synchronize motions with musical beats.
In contrast, our model achieves competitive rhythmic alignment without requiring strong beat-level supervision. Instead, we rely on a flexible multi-modal fusion framework that incorporates both musical and beat features in a weakly supervised manner. This enables our model to produce rhythmically aware motion while maintaining better generalization across genres and motion styles.

\noindent\textbf{Human Evaluation.}
To gain a deeper understanding of the visual and rhythmic quality of our method, we conduct a user study comparing the dance sequences generated by DanceChat with those produced by baseline methods on the AIST++ dataset and the in-the-wild musics. This study involves 30 participants, each engaging in individual evaluations.
Participants are shown a series of 20 pairs of comparison videos, each approximately 10 seconds in length. Each pair includes one video generated by DanceChat and one by a competing method, synchronized to the same music track. The participants’ task is to identify which of the two videos feel better.
The results of the user study on AIST++ are summarized in Table \ref{tab:user_study}. Notably, DanceChat outperformed all the baseline results. We also conduct a separate evaluation in an “in-the-wild” setting, using unseen music tracks to test DanceChat’s generalization capabilities. As shown in Table \ref{tab: in_the_wild}, DanceChat achieves a preference rate of 66.36\% over EDGE in these real-world scenarios, further confirming its robustness and adaptability across different musical contexts. 
In addition to the user study, an observable reduction in foot sliding is noted in the DanceChat videos, further confirming the PFC improvement.


\subsection{Ablation Study}
We perform an ablation study on the AIST++ dataset to evaluate the impact of different modalities (music, beat, and text) and the effectiveness of the multi-modal alignment loss in generating high-quality dance motions. The results, shown in Table \ref{tab:ablation}, are reported in terms of $\mathrm{PFC}$ (for motion realism), $\mathrm{DIV}_k$ (for motion diversity), and BAS (for beat alignment score), with lower $\mathrm{PFC}$ and higher $\mathrm{DIV}_k$ and BAS indicating better performance.

\noindent\textbf{Impact of Modality Inputs.}
To understand the individual contributions of each modality, we assess various configurations by selectively including or excluding the music (M), beat (B), and text (T) modalities. 
The baseline model, using only music (M), performs the lowest across all metrics, with limited diversity and weak rhythmic alignment. Adding beat information (M+B) enhances rhythmic alignment, indicating that beat cues contribute both to physical realism in generated movement patterns and to synchronization with the music.
Adding textual guidance (M+T) leads to the best performance in PFC and diversity, suggesting that text improves motion realism and expressiveness by providing explicit semantic choreography cues. However, lacking beat information, this configuration underperforms in BAS.
Interestingly, combining all three modalities (M+B+T) produces the highest BAS and consistently strong results in other metrics.
% When textual motion instruction is included with music (M+T), the model achieves the best results in motion quality, suggesting that text guidance improves the realism and expressiveness of the generated motions by providing richer movement instructions. However, the absence of beat cues leads to a moderate level of rhythmic alignment, highlighting the complementary role of beat information in achieving precise synchronization. However, combining all three modalities (M+B+T) does not yield the highest performance in any individual metric, suggesting that the integration of multiple modalities requires additional alignment mechanisms to be fully effective.

\noindent\textbf{Impact of Multi-Modal Alignment Loss.}
We further incorporate the multi-modal alignment loss (MM Align. Loss), which leverages text as an intermediary bridge between music and motion to narrow the semantic gap between these modalities. 
%With the alignment loss applied, the model achieves an $\mathrm{FID}_k$ of 31.18, $\mathrm{DIV}_k$ of 5.11, and BAS of 0.2448. 
% While the $\mathrm{FID}_k$ and BAS are slightly lower than the best scores achieved with individual modalities, this configuration achieves a balanced improvement across all metrics, indicating that 
The alignment loss leads to both improved $\mathrm{PFC}$) and $\mathrm{DIV}_k$, demonstrating its effectiveness in facilitating cross-modal integration. This suggests that using text as a bridge in the alignment loss helps create semantically rich and rhythmically aligned dance motions that benefit from the strengths of each modality without compromising diversity or quality.

