\section{Introduction}
LLMs have become the go-to solution for many tasks, showcasing promising results on standard benchmarks, potentially replacing humans across various domains~\citep{brown2020language, openai2023gpt4}. However, their reliability in tasks that require real attention to detail, such as tasks that directly impact human life, remains concerning~\citep{bommasani2021opportunities, zhang2023audit}. Medical tasks like clinical decision-making represent a critical domain where experts must possess comprehensive knowledge in cultural contexts, medical principles, pharmaceutical information, and numerous other specialized areas in healthcare~\citep{liu2023trustworthy, lee2023benefits}.


Although recent works have demonstrated that LLMs may achieve accuracy rates exceeding 90\% on English medical question-answering tasks~\citep{singhal2022large, nori2023capabilities}, their performance falls off significantly in other languages other than English~\citep{lee2023benefits,alghanem2023multilingual}. Importantly, simply translating questions is inadequate, as subtle cultural cues and localized standards of care often vanish in the process~\citep{joshi2020state,liu2023trustworthy}. These nuances can decisively alter diagnoses, treatment plans, and ultimately patient outcomes~\citep{vilares2023evaluation,min2023multilingual}.


For low-resource languages, the evidence base is even thinner. Limited research has investigated the specific factors that mislead LLMs in medical contexts, in multilingual and low-resource language settings like Persian. A deeper investigation on the medical sub-fields in which LLMs excel or underperform is essential for identifying suitable use cases and implementing necessary safeguards~\citep{ bommasani2021opportunities}. Such insights are crucial for the responsible deployment of LLMs in clinical environments, where errors carry substantial clinical risk~\citep{zhang2023audit, liu2023trustworthy}.

To fill this gap, we introduce \textbf{PersianMedQA}, a large-scale, expert-annotated dataset covering 23 medical specialties. The dataset includes a comprehensive bilingual dictionary of Persian and medical terms to support both evaluation and model adaptation. As a benchmark, we evaluate a range of state-of-the-art models, including general-purpose models, Persian fine-tuned models, and medical models in both Persian and English. Our experiments uncover a pronounced language gap between Persian and English: closed-source models such as GPT-4.1 significantly outperform open-source counterparts. Notably, Persian fine-tuned models exhibited minimal domain knowledge and performed the worst, while medical fine-tuned models showed only modest improvements and failed to generalize effectively to Persian clinical data. Figure~\ref{fig:paper-overview} illustrates the overall workflow of our study, including dataset collection, model evaluation, and analysis steps.

In Section~\ref{sec:related}, we review prior work on medical QA benchmarks, multilingual LLM evaluation, and Persian language models. Section~\ref{sec:construction} describes the PersianMedQA dataset construction, including data collection, cleaning, and annotation. Section~\ref{sec:experiments} presents our experimental setup and zero-shot, CoT, translation-impact, and ensembling evaluations, and the analysis of the results across medical subfields, model sizes, and artifact reliance. Finally, Section~\ref{sec:conclusion} concludes with key findings, limitations, and directions for future research.



\section{Related Works}
\label{sec:related}


\paragraph{Medical Question Answering (QA).}
Medical QA has long been used as a benchmark for machine reasoning in high-stakes domains. Progress accelerated with domain-specific language pre-training: \textsc{BioBERT}~\citep{lee2020biobert} and \textsc{PubMedBERT}~\citep{gu2021domain} each delivered sizable gains on benchmark datasets including \textsc{PubMedQA}~\citep{jin2019pubmedqa}, \textsc{MedQA}~\citep{jin2021disease}, and \textsc{MedMCQA}~\citep{pal2022medmcqa}. Recent retrieval-augmented generation approaches \textsc{(RAG)}~\citep{lewis2020retrieval} attempt to ground LLM outputs in trusted sources, yet factual consistency remains a challenge~\citep{singhal2022large}. Multilingual coverage is also expanding: the \textsc{CBM} benchmark~\citep{zhang2023cbm} introduces a comprehensive suite of Chinese medical QA tasks, underscoring the field’s rising attention to multilingual healthcare evaluation.


\paragraph{LLMs in Medical Practice.}
Medical-specific LLMs such as \textsc{Med-PaLM} and \textsc{Med-PaLM 2} have shown that combining domain-specific pre-training, instruction tuning, and CoT prompting can enhance performance on the United States Medical Licensing Examination (USMLE) well above the passing threshold~\citep{singhal2022large,singhal2023towards,wei2022chain}. Despite these advances, most published evaluations remain English-centric. While General-purpose models like \textsc{GPT-4} achieve strong zero-shot results on a variety of medical QA benchmarks~\citep{openai2023gpt4,nori2023capabilities,lee2023benefits}, their behavior in multilingual clinical settings, especially for low-resource languages like Persian, has not been systematically explored.



\paragraph{Multilingual Medical QA and the Limits of Translation.}
A common workaround for evaluating medical QA in low-resource languages is to translate questions into English. Yet recent work shows that this \emph{translation-first} pipeline can strip away critical terminology and distort local clinical guidelines, ultimately hurting accuracy and safety.  
At the same time, several multilingual, or at least non-English medical QA benchmarks have appeared, including MedQA (Chinese) \citep{jin2021disease}, MedMCQA (Hindi) \citep{pal2022medmcqa}, CBM (Chinese) \citep{zhang2023cbm}, and the aggregated MultiMedQA suite \citep{singhal2022large}. MedExpQA further augments these resources with cross-lingual explanations \citep{alonso2024medexpqa}.  Despite this progress, no publicly available dataset targets Persian medical QA, leaving its distinct clinical context completely unrepresented. These gaps highlight the need for language-aware medical LLMs and benchmarks rather than one-size-fits-all translation strategies.


\paragraph{Persian Language Models and QA.}
Efforts in Persian NLP have produced strong monolingual models such as ParsBERT~\citep{farahani2020parsbert}, Dorna~\citep{partai_dorna2_llama3.1_8b_instruct}. These models outperform multilingual baselines on tasks like sentiment analysis and classification. However, few are trained or evaluated in the medical domain. SINA-BERT~\citep{taghizadeh2021sina} mark early attempts to address this gap, yet focus on document classification or conversational QA. 


\section{PersianMedQA Construction}
\label{sec:construction}

The PersianMedQA dataset was developed by collecting 14 years of multiple-choice questions from the Iranian residency and pre-residency medical exams. Each item includes the question text, four answer options, and the correct answer key. Figure~\ref{fig:dataset-examples} presents representative examples of clinical and non-clinical questions. The raw dataset underwent a rigorous preprocessing pipeline to ensure quality, consistency, and relevance for multilingual medical QA evaluation. The following steps summarize the construction process:

