\end{table}


In high-stakes domains like medicine, it is preferable to abstain rather than to provide incorrect answers. To that end, we implement a confidence-based “selective answering” strategy. We embed each question with LaBSE, assign a pseudo-confidence equal to the mean accuracy of its three cosine-nearest neighbors, and answer only when this score exceeds a tunable threshold. We evaluate performance in terms of {\em accuracy} (on the answered subset) and {\em coverage} (the fraction of questions answered).
Aggregating across all models, selective answering yields substantial accuracy gains when partial coverage is acceptable. Table~\ref{tab:selective-sample} shows a small sample of models under this regime.
