The sampling of the ScanNet dataset consists of 50,000 points, with the quantized data approximately amounting to 20,000 points, and a portion of this distribution is somewhat uneven, placing these data between dense and sparse distributions. Therefore, traditional methods like G-PCC are more effective in restoring the original coordinates, while learning-based methods suffer from a degree of randomness in reconstruction due to the lack of constraints in the encoding transformation.





