As shown, human workers achieve higher scores than any type of cheater on average.
However, some workers fall below the average score of cheaters. 
Several factors contribute to this issue.
For example, a subset of workers may themselves be cheaters in the dataset, or they may have been assigned many tasks that are inherently difficult to classify.
Consequently, no detection method can achieve perfect accuracy. 
Next, we will examine how well our method distinguishes workers from cheaters compared to other baselines.



\subsubsection{Baseline Measurements}

We introduce the key ideas of the following four baselines.
\begin{enumerate}[topsep=0pt]
    \setlength{\itemsep}{1pt}
    \setlength{\parskip}{0pt}
    \item We consider the \textbf{Output Agreement (OA)} mechanism that directly counts the frequency of agreements between two agent's responses.
        Specifically, while scoring labeler $i$, we iteratively pair her with a different labeler $j$ and let $M_{i,j}$ denote the set of questions answered by both labelers.
        Then, the score of labeler $i$ is the average of the empirical probability of agreement, i.e.~$\frac{1}{n}\sum_{j\in[n]\backslash\{i\}}\sum_{q\in M_{i,j}} \frac{\mathbbm{1}\left[\hat{X}_{i,q} = \hat{X}_{j,q}\right]}{|M_{i,j}|}$, and let $\frac{0}{0} = 0$.
    \item We use the \textbf{Correlated Agreement (CA)} mechanism as a baseline, which is introduced in \cref{app:preliminary}.
          The expected CA score is the unconditioned mutual information $I_{\text{TVD}}(\hat{X}_i;\hat{X}_j)$.
    \item Another common method to estimate the reliability of the noisy agents is the \textbf{Expectation-Maximization (EM)} algorithm under the DS model \citep{dawid1979maximum}.
        Under the DS model, each agent $i$ makes mistakes according to a confusion matrix $\Gamma^i$ where $\Gamma^i_{h,l}$ denotes the probability that labeler $i$ reports $h$ when the ground truth answer is $l$.
        The EM algorithm first initializes a prior of the ground truth for each question and a confusion matrix for each agent.
        Then, it iteratively goes through an E-step and an M-step.
        In the E-step, the algorithm computes the posterior of the ground truth of each question given the prior and the confusion matrix. 
        In the M-step, the confusion matrix of each agent is updated using the posterior computed in the E-step.
        The above two steps are iterated until the likelihood of the data matrix $\hat{X}$ converges.
        Finally, we score the reliability of agent $i$ as $\sum_{h\in \Sigma} \Gamma^i_{h,h} \tilde{P}(h)$ where $\tilde{P}(h)$ is the empirical marginal distribution of observing $h$ in the dataset.
    \item Lastly, we consider the \textbf{Conditioned Agreement ($\text{OA}_{Z}$)} which counts the frequency of agreements only when the agreement is different from the principal's sample on that question.
        Similar to the agreement metric, the score of labeler $i$ is the average of the empirical probability of agreement conditioned on $Z$, i.e.~$\frac{1}{n}\sum_{j\in[n]\backslash\{i\}}\sum_{q\in M_{i,j}} \frac{\mathbbm{1}\left[\hat{X}_{i,q} = \hat{X}_{j,q} \& \hat{X}_{i,q} \neq Z_q\right]}{|M_{i,j}|}$.
        Intuitively, if labeler $i$ uses an LLM whose generated labels are similar to $Z$, then $\hat{X}_{i,q} \neq Z_q$ will happen with a small probability, meaning that the conditioned agreement score will be small.
\end{enumerate}

We note that there are many more heuristic methods to estimate the reliability of crowdsourcing agents \citep{4415269, embretson2013item, Piech2013}.
Many of these methods are variants of the EM algorithm under a different context-dependent model.
We nonetheless only use the EM algorithm with the DS model to represent this line of methods because they all ignore the principal's information $Z$ and thus are unlikely to perform well with LLM-corrupted datasets.


