\subsection{Generation of the Exploration}
According to Corollary \ref{connect_co}, under the condition that the graph $G(n,p)$ is connected, the distribution of the sequence $\{W_k\}$ coincides with the distribution of $\{X_k\}$, where the $X_k$ are independent random variables with $X_k \sim \mathrm{Poiss}(\lambda_k)$, conditioned on
$$
\sum_{t=1}^n X_t = n - 1,
\quad
S_k = \sum_{t=1}^k (X_t - 1) \ge 0,\quad k < n.
$$
A natural approach is to employ acceptance-rejection sampling.

The trajectory generation procedure described above is already performs well in practical settings. Nevertheless, it can be further accelerated by restricting the sampling to only those trajectories that satisfy the condition $S_n = -1$. This can be achieved by using the following identity in distribution:
\begin{equation*}
\begin{gathered}
\mathbf{P} \left( (X_{1}, \dotsc, X_{n}) = \mathbf{j} \ \Big| \ \sum_{t=1}^{n} X_t = n - 1 \right)
= \mathbf{P} \left( (Y_{1}, \dotsc, Y_{n}) = \mathbf{j} \right), \\
(Y_{1}, \dotsc, Y_{n}) \sim \mathrm{Multinomial}\left(n - 1;\, \tfrac{\lambda_1}{n}, \dotsc, \tfrac{\lambda_n}{n} \right).
\end{gathered}
\end{equation*}
This version is used for the first step of Algorithm~\ref{alg:connected-gnp}. Examples of random walks $S_k$, obtained by this method, are shown in Figure \ref{ex1}.
