Despite the asymmetry of the generation procedure, the final distribution of graphs turns out to be symmetric. In particular, distribution of a vertex degree does not depend on its index, as seen in Figure~\ref{fig:degree-vs-index}. The figure also shows the theoretical vertex degree ditribution discussed in Remark \ref{zeta_remark}.

\begin{Rem}\label{zeta_remark}
The number of neighbors of vertices in a connected graph corresponds to the distribution of the random variable $X_1$ conditioned on the walk $\{S_k\}$ being positive. The asymptotic distribution of this variable is approximately given by 
\begin{equation*}
\mathbf{P}(X_1 = k) \approx e^{-\gamma} \frac{\gamma^{k}}{k!} \frac{1-e^{-ck}}{1-e^{-c}}, \quad \gamma = \frac{c}{1-e^{-c}}.
\end{equation*}
A rigorous proof of this fact is nontrivial and is not provided here. However, it is clearly observed in our experiments. In particular, it implies the expression for the average degree of a vertex in a connected graph:
\begin{equation}\label{zeta}
\begin{gathered}
\zeta(c) = \sum_{k=0}^{\infty} k\, e^{-\gamma} \frac{\gamma^{k}}{k!} \frac{1-e^{-ck}}{1-e^{-c}} =
% \\
\frac{\gamma - \gamma e^{-c} \exp\bigl(\gamma(e^{-c}-1)\bigr)}{1-e^{-c}} =
c\frac{1+e^{-c}}{1-e^{-c}}.
\end{gathered}
\end{equation}
This expression will be used in practice when we construct the procedure for generating connected $G(n,m)$ graphs.
\end{Rem}
