\section{Generation of Connected Graphs $G(n,m)$}

\subsection{Generation Algorithm}\label{section:alg2}

In this section, we derive a method for generating random graphs of the $G(n,m)$ model under the connectivity condition. As in the case of $G(n,p)$, we are interested in exact generation, that is, a method that yields a uniform distribution over the set of all connected graphs with $n$ vertices and $m$ edges.

The method is based on the following idea: we use the already constructed exact algorithm for generating connected graphs $G(n,p)$, choosing the parameter $p$ appropriately. Then, we apply an acceptance-rejection procedure, accepting only those graphs that have exactly $m$ edges. The overall procedure is described in Algorithm~\ref{alg:connected-gnm}.
\par
\vspace{0.5em}
% \begin{figure}[H]
% \begin{minipage}{1.0\textwidth}
\LinesNumbered
\begin{algorithm}[H]
\DontPrintSemicolon
\caption{Generation of a Connected Graph $G(n,m)$}
\label{alg:connected-gnm}
\vspace{0.3em}
\KwIn{Number of vertices $n \ge 1$, number of edges $n-1 \leq m \leq n(n-1)/2$}
\KwOut{Connected graph $G$ from $G(n, m)$}
\vspace{0.3em}

\textbf{Step 0. Compute $p$} 

$\displaystyle p\gets c/n:\ \zeta(c) = 2m/(n-1)$
\vspace{0.3em}

\textbf{Step 1. Generation of the exploration trajectory} 

$\displaystyle \lambda_i \gets \frac{np}{1 - (1 - p)^n} (1 - p)^{i - 1}, \quad i = 1, \dots, n$

\Repeat{until $\displaystyle S_k \ge 0$ for all $k < n$ and $E_p = m - (n-1)$}
{
Generate $\displaystyle (X_1, \dots, X_n) 
\sim 
\mathrm{Multinomial}\left(n-1; \,\tfrac{\lambda_1}{n}, \dots, \tfrac{\lambda_n}{n}\right)$

$\displaystyle S_k \gets \sum_{i=1}^k (X_i - 1), \quad k = 1, \dots, n$

Generate $\displaystyle E_p \sim \mathrm{Binomial}\left(\sum_{i=1}^{n-1} S_i, \, p\right)$
}

\vspace{0.3em}

\textbf{Step 2. Construction of the exploration tree} 

$\displaystyle \mathcal{A}_1 \gets \{v_0\}, \quad \mathcal{U}_1 \gets V \setminus \{v_0\}$

\For{$t = 1$ \KwTo $n$}{
Select $\displaystyle v_t \in \mathcal{A}_t$

Select uniformly at random $\displaystyle \mathcal{W}_t \subset \mathcal{U}_t$, \ $|\mathcal{W}_t| = X_t$

Add edges $\displaystyle (v_t, w)$ to $E(G)$ for all $\displaystyle w \in \mathcal{W}_t$

Update $\displaystyle \mathcal{A}_{t+1}, \ \mathcal{U}_{t+1}$ according to (\ref{AUW})

}
\vspace{0.3em}

\textbf{Step 3. Addition of the remaining edges} 

$\displaystyle \mathcal{P} \gets \bigcup_{t} \{(v_t, w): w \in \mathcal{A}_t \setminus v_t\}$

Select uniformly at random a subset $\mathcal{M} \subseteq \mathcal{P}$ consisting of $m - (n-1)$ elements

\For{each pair $\displaystyle (u,v) \in \mathcal{M}$}{
    Add the edge $\displaystyle (u,v)$ to $E(G)$
}
\vspace{0.2em}
\Return{graph $\displaystyle G$}
\end{algorithm}
% \end{minipage}
% \end{figure}
\vspace{0.3em}

\subsection{Explanation of the Method's Correctness}

Consider a graph $g \sim G(n,p)$ for any fixed $p$. In this model, all graphs with the same number of edges $m$ have the same probability:
\[
\mathbf{P}_{G(n,p)}(g) = p^m (1 - p)^{\binom{n}{2} - m},\quad \text{if } |E(g)| = m.
\]
Thus, the conditional distribution
$$
\mathbf{P}_{G(n,p)}\Bigl(g \ \Bigl| \ g\text{ is connected},\ |E(g)| = m \Bigr)
$$
is uniform over the set of all connected graphs with $n$ vertices and $m$ edges. This is exactly the distribution of connected graphs in the $G(n,m)$ model. Hence, if we generate connected $G(n,p)$ graphs using Algorithm~\ref{alg:connected-gnp} until $|E(G)| = m$, we obtain the correct distribution. However, such a scheme may be inefficient without additional optimization:
\begin{enumerate}[1)]
    \item We can immediately (in Step 1) generate the number of edges $E_p$ that will be added in Step 3, since it is known that
    $$\displaystyle E_p \sim \mathrm{Binomial}\left(\sum_{i=1}^{n-1} S_i, \, p\right).
    $$
    In this way, we only repeat the operations of the first step, which in the typical case ($p=c/n$) has a complexity of $O(n)$. In Step 3, we then choose the already known number of edges uniformly from the set $\mathcal{P}$.
    \item For the algorithm to work correctly, we must reject all trajectories $\{S_k\}$ if the desired value of $E_p$ is not obtained. Therefore, we must reduce the number of regenerations by choosing an optimal $p$. It is proposed to choose the parameter $p = c/n$ such that $\zeta(c) = 2m/(n-1)$ (see Remark \ref{zeta_remark}). A comparison of the random $m$ obtained by this approach and by the naive approach $c = 2m/(n-1)$ is shown in Figure \ref{fig:gnm-ru}.
\end{enumerate}

